WN.lb(15,{"mt_yoI41SePkG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Football shirt numbers are which type of data?","choices":["Categorical","Numerical and discrete","Numerical and continuous"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the mean of two shirt numbers mean anything?","explain":"Shirt numbers are labels for players. Their mean has no meaning, so they are categorical data."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Age, given in whole years, is which type of data?","choices":["Continuous","Discrete","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is age counted or measured? Does rounding change what it is?","explain":"Age is time, which is measured, so it is continuous. Rounding to whole years is only how we record it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"UK shoe sizes go 5, 5½, 6, 6½, and so on. What type of data is shoe size?","choices":["Discrete","Continuous","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you buy a size 5.3 shoe?","explain":"Shoe sizes come in set steps, with no sizes in between, so the data is discrete. Foot length would be continuous."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A vertical line chart shows numbers of siblings, with a line at 0, 1, 2, and so on, up to {n}. How many lines are on the chart?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count from 0, not from 1. Is 0 siblings one of the values?","explain":"The values are 0 to {n}, which is {=n+1} values. Counting from 0 adds one more than {n}.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why should the bars touch on a chart of grouped heights?","choices":["Heights are continuous, so every value between groups exists","Touching bars look neater","There are more than 4 groups"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 150.0 cm a real height? Which group is it in?","explain":"Height is continuous, so there are no gaps in real values. The class 150 ≤ h < 160 starts where the last one stops."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Times from 10 s up to {=10+5*k} s are grouped in classes of width 5 s (10 ≤ t < 15, and so on). How many classes are there?","answer":"k","hint":"Work out the range of times, then divide by the class width.","explain":"The range is {=5*k} s, and {=5*k} ÷ 5 = {k} classes.","params":{"k":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which variable gives discrete data?","choices":["Number of pets at home","Mass of a school bag","Length of a leaf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one do you count, rather than measure?","explain":"Pets are counted in whole numbers, so the data is discrete. Mass and length are measured, so they are continuous."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The temperature is read every hour for one day. Which chart suits it best?","choices":["A line graph","A pie chart","A bar chart with gaps"],"answer":0,"hint":"The temperature changes smoothly between the readings.","explain":"A line graph shows readings over time, and the line suggests the values in between. A pie chart needs parts of a whole."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Types of data: a survey records the reaction time of each pupil in seconds. What type of data is this?","choices":["Numerical and continuous","Numerical and discrete","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is time counted or measured? Could there be a value between 0.31 s and 0.32 s?","explain":"Time is measured, and 0.315 s lies between 0.31 s and 0.32 s. So reaction time is continuous numerical data."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A survey has {n} questions. 3 of them give categorical data. The rest give numerical data. How many questions give numerical data?","answer":"n-3","hint":"Every question gives either categorical or numerical data. Take the 3 categorical ones away from {n}.","explain":"{n} − 3 = {=n-3}. Each variable is one type or the other, so the rest are numerical.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which chart suits the favourite sport of each pupil in a class?","choices":["A bar chart with gaps","A histogram with touching bars","A line graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sports are separate names. Does a value exist between football and tennis?","explain":"Sports are categories with nothing in between, so the bars should have gaps. A pie chart would also work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Heights from 130 cm up to {=130+10*k} cm are grouped in classes of width 10 cm (130 ≤ h < 140, and so on). How many classes are there?","answer":"k","hint":"Find the total range, then divide by the class width of 10 cm.","explain":"The range is {=10*k} cm, and {=10*k} ÷ 10 = {k} classes of width 10 cm.","params":{"k":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A variable is one thing you collect data about, like eye colour or height. First ask: are the values names or numbers? Eye colour gives blue, brown and green. Those are names, so the data is categorical.","Some numbers are only labels. A football shirt number or a bus route number names something. A quick test helps: does a mean make sense? The mean of shirt 7 and shirt 9 is not a shirt 8 player. So shirt numbers are categorical too.","Numerical data splits two ways. Discrete data is counted, or it comes in set steps. You can have 2 or 3 siblings, but never 2.5. Continuous data is measured. Between 152.5 cm and 152.6 cm there is 152.55 cm, and you can keep zooming in. Height, mass, time and age are continuous.","Rounding does not change the type. We say age in whole years, but age is time, so it is continuous. Now pick the chart. Use a bar chart with gaps, or a pie chart, for categories. Use bars with gaps for discrete data. Use touching bars, a histogram, for grouped continuous data like heights in groups of 10 cm. Use a line graph for readings over time."],"adult":["This is KS3 statistics in the English national curriculum. Pupils draw and read tables, bar charts, pie charts and pictograms for categorical data. They use vertical line or bar charts for ungrouped and grouped numerical data. They compare discrete, continuous and grouped data. The same terms appear in Common Core grade 6 statistics, and GCSE assumes them.","Key terms: a variable is the thing you record. Categorical (qualitative) data are names or labels. Some are ordinal, with an order, like small, medium and large. Numerical (quantitative) data are amounts. Discrete data take separate values, and are often counts. Continuous data are measured, and can take any value in a range. Grouped continuous data uses class intervals written as inequalities, such as 150 ≤ h < 160. That way each value falls in exactly one class.","Common mistakes: calling any number numerical, when shirt and phone numbers are labels. Calling age or time discrete because it was rounded. Calling shoe size continuous, when sizes come in set steps (foot length is continuous). Drawing grouped heights with gaps between bars, as if 150.0 cm could not exist. Be careful with the word histogram. At KS3, touching bars with equal class widths are often called a frequency diagram. A true histogram, at GCSE Higher, plots frequency density.","In the lab, students sort variables with the mean test. They zoom into the gap between two heights, while the siblings line has nothing in between. They sort numbers into discrete or continuous, and try each chart on four data sets to see why it fits. Last, they run survey questions down a data tree."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, collect one bit of data about each person: favourite fruit, number of cousins, and hand span in cm. Ask which is categorical, which is discrete and which is continuous, and how you would draw each one.","keyIdea":"Data is categorical when the values are names or labels, and numerical when the values are amounts you can work with. Numerical data is discrete when it is counted or comes in set steps, and continuous when it is measured and can take any value in a range. The type decides the chart: gaps between bars for categories and discrete values, touching bars for grouped continuous data, and a line for readings over time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What type of data is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷️ Categorical","🔢 Discrete","📏 Continuous"],"items":[["Favourite fruit",0],["Number of cousins",1],["Hand span in cm",2],["Bus route number",0],["Goals scored in a match",1],["Time to run 100 m",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdtype","caption":"Flagship lab: sort variables, zoom into the gap between two values, pick a chart for each data set, and use a data tree.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QPcvhTzMmD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A dot plot of quiz scores has no dots above 7, but dots on both sides. What is the best description?","choices":["A gap: no one in this data scored 7","Scoring 7 is impossible","7 is an outlier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what an empty spot inside the data means, and what it does not mean.","explain":"It is a gap. No one in this group scored 7, but another group could."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do the bars of a histogram touch?","choices":["The classes join with no gaps between them","To save space on the page","Because the data is categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about times such as 29.9 s and 30 s. Is there any space between the classes?","explain":"Histograms show continuous (or grouped) numerical data. One class ends where the next starts, so the bars touch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You record the heights of 300 pupils. Which display shows the shape most clearly?","choices":["A histogram with equal-width classes","A dot plot with one dot for each pupil","A histogram with a single 100 cm wide bar"],"answer":0,"hint":"300 dots make very tall stacks; one huge bar hides everything.","explain":"Grouping into sensible equal classes shows the distribution. 300 dots are too many, and one bar shows no shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Travel times cluster from 5 to 10 minutes, and one pupil took 45 minutes. What is 45?","choices":["An outlier","The peak","A gap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 45 near the other values or far away from them?","explain":"45 is far from the cluster at 5–10, so it is an outlier. It is real data, so check it, but do not delete it without a reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Classes are 0 ≤ t < 10, 10 ≤ t < 20, 20 ≤ t < 30 with frequencies 4, 11, 6. What is the modal class?","choices":["10 ≤ t < 20","11","20 ≤ t < 30"],"answer":0,"hint":"The modal class is a class, not a number of values. Find the tallest bar.","explain":"11 is the highest frequency, so 10 ≤ t < 20 is the modal class."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The same 40 heights are drawn with classes 2 cm wide and with classes 20 cm wide. What happens with the 20 cm classes?","choices":["Only two bars, so the shape is hidden","More detail appears","The total frequency changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many 20 cm classes fit between 130 and 170 cm?","explain":"40 ÷ 20 = 2 bars. The total is still 40, but the peak and spread disappear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A dot plot has {n} dots. {k} of them sit at the peak value. How many dots are at other values?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each dot is one value. Take the dots at the peak away from the total.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} dots at other values.","params":{"n":[15,30],"k":[4,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Times are grouped into classes 10 s wide: 0 ≤ t < 10, 10 ≤ t < 20, and so on. Every time is less than {k} × 10 s, and some are just under it. How many classes do you need?","answer":"k","hint":"Each class covers 10 s. How many lots of 10 s reach from 0 up to {k} × 10 s?","explain":"The times run from 0 up to {=10*k} s, and {=10*k} ÷ 10 = {k}, so you need {k} classes and {k} touching bars.","params":{"k":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A dot plot shows stacks of 2, 5, 3 and 1 dots above 0, 1, 2 and 3 pets. What is the mode?","choices":["1 pet","5 pets","3 pets"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mode is the value under the tallest stack, not the number of dots in it.","explain":"The tallest stack has 5 dots and it sits above 1 pet, so the mode is 1 pet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A histogram of heights has {n} equal classes, each 5 cm wide. The first class starts at 130 cm. At what height in cm does the last class end?","answer":"130+5*n","hint":"Each class adds 5 cm. Work out how far {n} classes of 5 cm reach from 130.","explain":"{n} × 5 = {=5*n} cm, and 130 + {=5*n} = {=130+5*n} cm. Equal widths mean the classes step along evenly.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The classes are 20 ≤ t < 30 and 30 ≤ t < 40. A runner's time is exactly 30 s. Which class does it go in?","choices":["30 ≤ t < 40","20 ≤ t < 30","Both classes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the inequality signs. Which class can include 30 itself?","explain":"20 ≤ t < 30 stops just below 30. 30 ≤ t < 40 includes 30, so the time goes there, and only there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A histogram has bars of frequency {a}, {b} and 6. How many values does it show in total?","answer":"a+b+6","hint":"Each bar height is the number of values in that class. Add them.","explain":"{a} + {b} + 6 = {=a+b+6} values altogether.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[3,12]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A dot plot is a number line with one dot for every value. Ten football matches give ten dots. Dots with the same value stack up, so the tallest stack shows the most common value: the mode, or peak.","Then read the shape. A cluster is a group of values close together. A gap is a stretch of the number line inside the data with no dots. An outlier is a value far away from the rest. A gap does not mean a value is impossible; it means no one in this data had it.","With 300 heights a dot plot becomes a forest of towers. A histogram groups the values into classes of equal width, such as 140 ≤ h < 150 cm, and draws one bar per class. The height of the bar is the frequency: how many values fall in that class.","The bars touch because the classes join with no gaps between them: a height can be 149.9 cm or 150 cm. Each value goes in exactly one class, and a boundary value goes in the class it starts. Very thin classes make a spiky picture; very wide ones hide the shape in a couple of bars."],"adult":["Curriculum links: Common Core 6.SP.4 asks students to display numerical data in plots on a number line, including dot plots, histograms and box plots, and 6.SP.2 and 6.SP.5 to describe a distribution by its centre, spread and overall shape. In England, KS3 statistics asks pupils to construct and interpret appropriate tables, charts and diagrams, including grouped frequency tables for continuous data, which leads to GCSE histograms (where unequal widths use frequency density; this lesson keeps to equal widths, so frequency is the bar height).","Key terms: distribution, frequency, class (class interval), class width, class boundary, mode and modal class, cluster, gap, peak, outlier, discrete and continuous data. Writing classes as inequalities such as 20 ≤ t < 30 makes it clear which class a boundary value belongs to.","Common mistakes: reading the height of a stack as the value (saying the peak is 6 when 6 is how many dots sit at 2 goals), treating a gap as an impossible value, putting a boundary value such as 30 s in the class that ends at 30, drawing gaps between histogram bars as if the data were categories, and being careful about class width, since very wide classes can hide two peaks.","In the lab students plot ten match results as stacked dots, find a peak, a gap and an outlier in travel times, change the class width of a histogram of 40 heights from 2 to 20 cm and compare shapes, drag bar tops to build a histogram from a frequency table, then read the modal class and place a boundary value."]},"familyTip":"Ask everyone at home how many minutes it took them to get ready this morning, and draw a dot plot on paper. Is there a cluster, a gap or an outlier? Then group the times into 5-minute classes and draw touching bars.","keyIdea":"A dot plot puts one dot for each value above a number line, so you can see every value and describe the shape: clusters, gaps, peaks and outliers. When there are many values, or the data is continuous, a histogram groups them into classes of equal width; the bar height is the frequency, and the bars touch because each class runs straight into the next (20 ≤ t < 30, then 30 ≤ t < 40). The class width you choose decides how much of the shape you can see.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which chart suits the data? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📍 Dot plot","📊 Histogram"],"items":[["Goals in 10 matches",0],["Heights of 300 pupils",1],["Shoe sizes of 15 friends",0],["Reaction times of 80 people",1],["Pets owned by 20 pupils",0],["Masses of 120 apples",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghist","caption":"Flagship lab: stack dots, find peaks, gaps and outliers, change class width, build a histogram from a table and read it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_50bMWX7wRf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is How many legs does a spider have? not a statistical question?","choices":["Every answer is the same, 8","It is about animals","It has a number answer"],"answer":0,"hint":"A statistical question expects the answers to vary.","explain":"Spiders have 8 legs, so every answer is 8 and nothing varies. Number answers can still be statistical if they vary."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Data: 1, 2, 2, 3, 7. What is the median?","choices":["2","3","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sort the values and pick the middle one.","explain":"The middle of 1, 2, 2, 3, 7 is 2. The mean is 3 and the range is 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which change makes a question statistical?","choices":["How tall am I? becomes How tall are pupils in my class?","How tall am I? becomes How tall am I in metres?","How tall am I? becomes How tall was I last year?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a version where many different answers are expected.","explain":"Asking a whole class gives many heights that vary. The other versions still have one answer each."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two data sets both have a mean of 10. Set P has range 2 and set Q has range 15. Which is more consistent?","choices":["P","Q","They are equally consistent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consistent means the values are close together.","explain":"P's values sit within 2 of each other, so P is more consistent. The equal means hide the difference in spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Pupils' travel times to school range from {a} minutes to {b} minutes. What is the range?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract the smallest time from the largest.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} minutes.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[15,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which describes the spread of a data set?","choices":["The range","The median","The mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Centre is a typical value; spread is how far apart values are.","explain":"The range measures spread. The mean and median are measures of centre."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Five pupils each scored {s} on a quiz. What is the mean score?","answer":"s","hint":"If every value is the same, the typical value is that value.","explain":"Five scores of {s} add to {=5*s}, and {=5*s} ÷ 5 = {s}. The range is 0.","params":{"s":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Most dots on a plot sit at 1 and 2, with a few out at 9 and 10. What does this tell you about its shape?","choices":["It is skewed: most values are low with a long tail to the right","It is symmetric","It has no spread"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are most of the dots, and where does the tail go?","explain":"The pile sits on the left and a tail stretches right, so the distribution is skewed, and the mean is pulled above the median."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A data set is {k}, {k}, {k}, {k}+{d}. What is the range?","answer":"d","hint":"Range is the largest value minus the smallest value.","explain":"Largest {=k+d} minus smallest {k} gives a range of {d}.","params":{"k":[2,6],"d":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a statistical question?","choices":["How many minutes do pupils in Year 7 spend on homework each night?","How many minutes are in an hour?","How many minutes did I spend on homework last night?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would different people, or different times, give different answers?","explain":"Homework time varies from pupil to pupil, so we expect variability. An hour always has 60 minutes, and one person on one night gives one answer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A dot plot shows the number of pets owned by pupils. The smallest value is {a} and the largest is {a}+{b}. What is the range?","answer":"b","hint":"Range = largest value − smallest value.","explain":"Range = ({a}+{b}) − {a} = {b}. The range measures spread, not the size of the values.","params":{"a":[0,3],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Class A scored 6, 7, 7, 8 and Class B scored 2, 5, 9, 12. Both have a mean of 7. What is different?","choices":["The spread: Class B's scores are more spread out","The centre: Class B scored higher","Nothing, the data is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find each range: largest minus smallest.","explain":"Class A has range 2 and Class B has range 10. Same centre, very different spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Every one of {n} pupils is asked how many wheels a bicycle has, and each says 2. What is the range of the answers?","answer":"0","hint":"If every answer is the same, how far apart are the largest and smallest?","explain":"All {n} answers are 2, so the range is 2 − 2 = 0. No variability means the question is not statistical.","params":{"n":[5,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some questions have one fixed answer. How many days are in a week? Everyone says 7. Other questions get different answers from different people, like How many pets do you have? Those are statistical questions.","A statistical question expects variability. That means the answers vary. Having a number answer is not enough. How old am I? gives a number, but there is only one answer, so it is not statistical.","When you collect the answers, they form a distribution. You can show it on a dot plot. The centre is a typical value, like the mean or the median. The spread tells you how far apart the values are. The range is the largest value take away the smallest.","Two sets of data can share a centre but have very different spreads. Scores of 4, 5, 5, 5, 6 and scores of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 both have a mean of 5. The first set is packed close together. The second is spread out."],"adult":["This topic matches Common Core 6.SP.1 and 6.SP.2: recognise a statistical question as one that anticipates variability in the data, and understand that a data set has a distribution described by its centre, spread and overall shape. In England the same ideas sit in Key Stage 3 statistics, where pupils describe and compare distributions using averages and the range.","Variability can come from asking many people (heights in a class) or from measuring one thing many times (how long my walk to school takes each day). The test is whether repeated answers are expected to differ, not what the question is about.","Common mistakes: thinking any question with a number answer is statistical. Calling a question about one person statistical because the topic, such as age, could vary. Using only the mean to compare two groups and ignoring spread. Mixing up the range with the largest value, or the median with the mean.","In the lab, learners sort six questions and collect answers onto dot plots to see a stack with range 0 next to a spread of answers. They drag dots to reach goals, such as range 0 with mean 5 or range 8 with the same mean, while mean, median and range update live. Last, they compare two classes by centre and spread."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, ask a question everyone answers, such as How many hours did you sleep last night? Write the answers down and talk about the middle value and how far apart the answers are. Then ask What day is it today? and notice why that one has no spread.","keyIdea":"A statistical question is one that expects variability in the answers: How many pets do pupils in my class have? is statistical, but How old am I? has one answer and is not. The data collected to answer a statistical question has a distribution, which can be described by its centre (such as the mean or median), its spread (such as the range) and its overall shape.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Statistical or not? Drag each question to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Statistical question","Not statistical"],"items":[["How tall are the pupils in my class?",0],["How tall is the school door?",1],["How many books did each pupil read this term?",0],["How many sides does a square have?",1],["What time do pupils go to bed?",0],["What year is it?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagspread","caption":"Flagship lab: sort statistical questions, collect answers onto dot plots, drag dots to change centre and spread, then compare two classes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_c29FaCTNsx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tally has {g} gates of 5 and {s} more single lines. How many is that?","answer":"5*g+s","hint":"Count the gates in 5s, then count on the single lines.","explain":"{g} gates make {=5*g}. Count on {s} more to get {=5*g+s}.","params":{"g":[1,3],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. Cats have 4 pictures. How many votes for cats?","choices":["8","4","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the key before you count.","explain":"Each picture is 2 votes, so count in 2s: 2, 4, 6, 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In a block diagram, which column shows the most popular?","choices":["The tallest one","The first one","The shortest one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each block is one vote, so more blocks means more votes.","explain":"The tallest column has the most blocks, so it has the most votes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Walk has 6 votes. Bus has 2. How many more for walk?","choices":["4","8","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many more means find the difference, not add.","explain":"6 take away 2 is 4, so 4 more children walk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. What does half a picture mean?","choices":["1 vote","2 votes","Half a child"],"answer":0,"hint":"A whole picture is 2 votes. What is half of 2?","explain":"Half of 2 is 1, so half a picture means 1 vote."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Apples got {a} votes and pears got {b} votes. How many votes in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"In all means add the two totals together.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} votes in all.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do we cross the 5th tally mark?","choices":["So we can count in 5s","To show a mistake","Because 5 is the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about counting lots of tally marks quickly.","explain":"A gate of 5 lets you count 5, 10, 15 instead of counting every line."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Each picture means 1 vote. Bananas have 3 pictures. How many tally marks should bananas have?","choices":["3","1","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tally chart and a pictogram show the same counts.","explain":"One picture for each vote, so 3 pictures match 3 tally marks."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How do you draw 5 with tally marks?","choices":["4 lines and 1 line across them","5 lines standing up","The number 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 5th line does something different from the first 4.","explain":"The 5th line goes across the other 4. That makes a gate of 5 that is quick to count."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Each picture in a pictogram means 2 votes. A row has {n} pictures. How many votes is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in 2s, once for each picture.","explain":"{n} pictures of 2 votes each is {=2*n} votes. Count in 2s.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A block diagram shows {a} children like red and {b} like blue. How many more like red than blue?","answer":"a-b","hint":"How many more means find the difference. Take the smaller number from the bigger one.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} more children like red.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. A row has 3 whole pictures and a half picture. How many votes?","choices":["7","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 2s for the whole pictures. Half of 2 is 1.","explain":"3 whole pictures are 2, 4, 6, and the half picture is 1 more, so 7 votes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Tally marks help you count. Draw one line for each vote. The 5th line goes across the other 4. That makes a gate. A gate is 5.","A pictogram uses pictures. Look at the key first. If one picture means 1, count the pictures. If one picture means 2, count in 2s. Half a picture means 1.","A block diagram has one block for each vote. The tallest column has the most votes. The shortest has the fewest.","How many more? Find the difference. Walk 6, car 4. Take 4 from 6. That is 2 more."],"adult":["In England, Year 2 statistics asks children to interpret and construct simple pictograms, tally charts, block diagrams and simple tables, to ask and answer simple questions by counting the number of objects in each category and sorting the categories by quantity, and to ask and answer questions about totalling and comparing categorical data. Year 3 moves to pictograms where one symbol stands for more than one, such as 2, 5 or 10. In the US, Common Core 2.MD.D.10 asks for picture graphs and bar graphs with a single-unit scale.","The ideas underneath: a tally is a running count, and the gate of five lets you count in 5s instead of in 1s. In a pictogram the key is the scale. With a key of 2, a row of 4 pictures means 8, and half a picture means 1. A block diagram is an early bar chart: each block is one item, so the height of a column shows its total, and two columns can be compared block by block.","Common mistakes to watch for: drawing five upright lines instead of four and a line across; counting the pictures and forgetting the key, so 4 pictures read as 4 instead of 8; treating half a picture as a whole one; adding the two numbers when asked how many more; and reading the most popular as the first row rather than the longest one.","In the lab your child tallies 11 fruit votes into gates of 5, turns the tally into a pictogram, builds a pictogram where each picture means 2 with a half picture for 1, stacks a block diagram from a tally, and finds how many more by spotting the blocks with no partner. Wrong taps get feedback that names the slip."]},"familyTip":"Count something together this week, such as the colours of cars that pass or the fruit in the bowl, and keep a tally on paper in gates of 5. Then draw it as a pictogram and ask: which had the most, and how many more than the least?","keyIdea":"A tally chart counts as you go: one line for each thing, and the 5th line goes across the other 4 to make a gate of 5, so you can count in 5s. A pictogram shows the same data with pictures. The key tells you what one picture means: if each picture means 2, count in 2s, and half a picture means 1. A block diagram stacks one block for each thing, so the tallest column is the most popular. To find how many more, find the difference between two totals: take the smaller from the bigger.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Tally chart, pictogram or block diagram? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Tally chart","Pictogram","Block diagram"],"items":[["Lines in gates of 5",0],["Count as each vote comes in",0],["Pictures in rows",1],["Has a key: each picture means 2",1],["One block for each vote, in columns",2],["The tallest column has the most",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtally","caption":"Flagship lab: tally votes in gates of 5, build pictograms with keys of 1 and 2, stack a block diagram and find how many more.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_R-CEmul2Ws":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a census?","choices":["Collecting data from every member of the population","Asking a random sample","A kind of graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"The UK holds one every ten years and asks every household.","explain":"A census collects data from the whole population. A sample collects data from part of it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What makes a sample random?","choices":["Every member has the same chance of being picked","You pick whoever is nearby","People choose to join"],"answer":0,"hint":"Random in statistics means fair chance, not just any old way.","explain":"In a random sample every member of the population has the same chance of being chosen, so no group is left out on purpose."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"In a random sample of 50 people, {y} like a new park design. Estimate how many of 1,000 residents like it.","answer":"y*20","hint":"1,000 is 20 times 50, so scale the sample count up by 20.","explain":"{y} × 20 = {=y*20}, so about {=y*20} of the 1,000 residents like the design.","params":{"y":[10,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Random samples of 10 students say {a}% and {b}% yes. What is the range of these two estimates, in percentage points?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the smaller estimate from the larger one.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} percentage points. Small samples vary a lot, so a bigger sample would give a smaller spread.","params":{"a":[60,80],"b":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"To find how much exercise teenagers in a town do, Ali asks people leaving the gym. What is the problem?","choices":["People at the gym exercise more than most","He should ask adults","The sample is random"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the gym crowd like all the teenagers in the town?","explain":"Gym users are not representative of all teenagers: they exercise more, so the sample is biased upward."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which sample of a 1,200-student school is likely to be best?","choices":["100 students picked at random from the full list","400 students from Year 11","The first 100 to arrive"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bigger sample from one year group is still only one year group.","explain":"100 picked at random from the full list is representative. 400 from Year 11 is bigger but biased towards one year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two random samples of 40 students give 45% and 55%. What does this show?","choices":["Random samples vary; the true value is probably near 50%","One sample must be biased","Sampling never works"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do two fair samples have to give the same answer?","explain":"This is sampling variability. Fair samples differ a little, and both are close to the true value, which is probably near 50%."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why might a company test 50 light bulbs from a batch instead of all of them?","choices":["Testing destroys the bulbs, so a census would leave none to sell","Samples are always more accurate","Bulbs cannot be counted"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a bulb when you test how long it lasts?","explain":"Testing until a bulb fails uses it up, so a census is impossible here. A random sample of 50 estimates the whole batch."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A school asks 60 of its 900 students about lunch. What is the population?","choices":["All 900 students","The 60 students asked","The lunch menu"],"answer":0,"hint":"The population is the whole group you want to know about.","explain":"The school wants to know about all 900 students, so they are the population. The 60 asked are the sample."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a random sample of 20 students, {y} say yes. What percentage of the sample is that?","answer":"y*5","hint":"Each student is 1 out of 20, which is 5%.","explain":"{y} out of 20 is {y} × 5% = {=y*5}%, so the estimate for the whole population is about {=y*5}% yes.","params":{"y":[4,16]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A random sample says {p}% of students walk to school. The school has 600 students. About how many walk?","answer":"p*6","hint":"1% of 600 is 6, so multiply the percentage by 6.","explain":"{p}% of 600 = {p} × 6 = {=p*6}, so about {=p*6} students walk. It is an estimate, because the sample can be a bit off.","params":{"p":[20,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A website poll lets anyone vote, and 2,000 people do. Why could it be biased?","choices":["People who care most choose to vote","2,000 is too few","Websites cannot count votes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who decides to answer a poll that anyone can join.","explain":"A voluntary poll is answered by people with strong views, so it leans one way however many vote."}],"explain":{"kid":["The population is everyone you want to know about. If you want to know what Hilltop students think, the population is all 240 of them. Asking all of them is called a census.","A census can take too long or cost too much, so we often ask a sample: a smaller group taken from the population. Then we use the sample to estimate the answer for everyone.","A sample only tells the truth if it is like the population. Asking only your friends, one class or people who choose to answer gives a biased sample. It leans one way.","In a random sample, every person has the same chance of being picked. Random samples still differ a bit from each other, but they scatter around the true answer. Bigger random samples land closer. A bigger biased sample is still biased."],"adult":["This matches the US Common Core standard 7.SP.A.1: a sample can be used to generalise about a population only if it is representative, and random sampling tends to give representative samples. It also matches the GCSE Mathematics content in England, which asks pupils to infer properties of a population from a sample while knowing the limits of sampling.","Key terms are population, census, sample, random sample, representative and bias. Sampling variability is the natural spread between random samples of the same size. It shrinks as the sample grows, which is why larger random samples give steadier estimates.","Common mistakes: thinking a big sample is always a good one; trusting a voluntary response poll because many people answered; confusing the population with the sample; and expecting a random sample to give the exact true value. Be careful with the word random, which in daily speech means haphazard, not a fair chance for all.","In the lab your learner sorts groups into population and sample, drags a box to take handy samples from one corner of a school, draws random samples that scatter round the true 50 percent, compares sample sizes with a big but biased sample, and judges four ways of choosing students."]},"familyTip":"When you see a poll in the news or online, ask two questions together: who was the population, and how was the sample chosen? A phone-in or website poll lets people choose to answer, so it is biased. A good poll says how many were asked and that they were chosen at random.","keyIdea":"The population is the whole group you want to know about. A census asks all of it; a sample asks part of it and uses the answers to estimate the whole. A sample only works if it is representative. A random sample gives everyone the same chance and scatters around the truth. A biased sample, such as volunteers or one handy group, leans one way, and making it bigger does not fix that.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A survey of all the students in a school: is each sample random, or biased? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Random sample","Biased sample"],"items":[["30 names drawn by a computer from the full school list",0],["Every 10th name on the full register",0],["The first 30 students through the gate",1],["Students who reply to an email asking for volunteers",1],["Everyone in the chess club",1],["Numbers picked from a hat with one number per student",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsample","caption":"Flagship lab: sort population and sample, drag a handy sample box, take random samples of different sizes, and judge four ways of choosing.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_xppl18avyY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"4 animals and 3 vehicles. Animals count is…","choices":["4","3","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the tally for the animals box only.","explain":"The animals category has 4 members, so its count is 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Most to least here is…","choices":["Animals then Vehicles","Vehicles then Animals","Same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare 4 with 3, then list bigger first.","explain":"Animals (4) outnumber vehicles (3), so animals come first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a mix-up?","choices":["Sort but never count the groups","Use the given boxes","Order groups by count"],"answer":0,"hint":"Skipping the tally breaks K.MD.3.","explain":"Sorting without counting leaves out the required tallies."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sorting into categories means…","choices":["use given groups, then count","only invent a colour rule","never compare group sizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Labels are given; counts come next.","explain":"Classify into given categories, then count and compare."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Category A has {n} items. What tally do you write?","answer":"n","hint":"Write the number of items you counted.","explain":"The tally for that category is {=n}.","params":{"n":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Two categories have {a} and {b}. What is the total sorted?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add both category counts together.","explain":"All sorted objects total {=a+b} when groups are {a} and {b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"3 red and 5 blue. Most is…","choices":["blue (5)","red (3)","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Larger tally wins when ordering by count.","explain":"Blue’s count of 5 is greater than red’s 3."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: finish a category sort.","items":["read the given labels","sort every object","say which group has more"],"hint":"Labels, then place, then compare counts.","explain":"Use given categories, classify all items, then compare tallies."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A bus goes in…","choices":["Vehicles","Animals","Either box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the given category name that fits a bus.","explain":"A bus is a vehicle, so it belongs in the Vehicles category."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"After sorting you should…","choices":["count each box","throw the boxes away","only look at colours"],"answer":0,"hint":"K.MD.3 asks for counts in each category.","explain":"Classify first, then count the objects in each category."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A box holds {n} toys. What is the count for that category?","answer":"n","hint":"The count equals how many objects sit in the box.","explain":"If the category has {n} objects, its count is {=n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Category A has {a} and B has {b} (A is larger). How many more in A?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the smaller tally from the larger tally carefully.","explain":"When A has {a} and B has {b}, A has {=a-b} more.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the sorting steps.","items":["put items in given boxes","count each category","order categories by count"],"hint":"Classify, then tally, then compare sizes.","explain":"Given categories first, then counts, then order by those counts."}],"explain":{"kid":["Someone gives you the boxes. Put each thing in the right box.","Count how many are in each box. Say the number out loud.","Which box has more? Put the bigger group first when you order by count.","Sorting is not finished until you can say how many are in each category."],"adult":["K.MD.3: put objects into given groups, count each group, then order the groups by count. Words: group, count, most, least.","Common mistakes: ignoring the given labels; sorting but never counting; comparing without counts; mixing this up with free colour sorting.","In the lab your child sorts animals and vehicles, sets counts 4 and 3, orders most to least, and spots sorting without counting."]},"familyTip":"Give a mixed pile of toys with two labels (animals / vehicles). Sort, count each box, then say which has more.","keyIdea":"Put objects into the given groups, count how many are in each group, then order the groups from most to least.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair sorting idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["put each item in a given box",0],["count how many in each box",0],["order groups from most to least",0],["never count after sorting",1],["ignore the given labels",1],["compare category sizes",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcatct","caption":"Flagship lab: sort into Animals/Vehicles, count each, order by count, catch mix-ups.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NWUGQ7J3Or":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why take the absolute value when finding deviations for MAD?","choices":["so distances above and below the mean do not cancel","so the MAD equals the range","so the MAD equals the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Without absolute value, signed deviations sum to zero about the mean.","explain":"Absolute value keeps distance. Signed deviations about the mean cancel; MAD would wrongly become 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["MAD is the same as the range","MAD averages |value − mean|","Same mean can hide different MAD"],"answer":0,"hint":"Range uses only the ends; MAD uses every value.","explain":"Range = max − min. MAD averages absolute deviations from the mean — different measure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Data 3, 5, 7. Mean is 5. Absolute deviations are…","choices":["2, 0, 2","3, 5, 7","0, 0, 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compute |3−5|, |5−5| and |7−5| carefully.","explain":"|3−5|=2, |5−5|=0, |7−5|=2. Then MAD = (2+0+2)/3 = 4/3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Absolute deviations 1, 1, 1, {a}. MAD = (1+1+1+{a})/4. Enter that MAD when a={a}.","answer":"(3+a)/4","hint":"Add the four absolute deviations, then divide by 4.","explain":"MAD = (3+{a})/4 = {=(3+a)/4}.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A bigger MAD means…","choices":["values sit farther from the mean on average","the mean is larger","the range must be zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"MAD measures typical distance from the centre.","explain":"Larger MAD → typically farther from the mean. It does not force the mean to grow or the range to vanish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Correct MAD steps start with…","choices":["find the mean → absolute deviations → average them","find the range → call it MAD","add the raw values and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Centre first, then distances, then average.","explain":"Mean, then |x − mean| for each x, then average those absolute deviations."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Data {a}, {a}+2. Mean = {a}+1. Absolute deviations are both 1. MAD?","answer":"1","hint":"Two absolute deviations of 1 each — average them.","explain":"MAD = (1+1)/2 = 1. Both values sit 1 unit from the mean {a}+1.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Range of 2, 5, 9 is 7. MAD of the same set is…","choices":["not necessarily 7 — range and MAD are different measures","always equal to the range","always equal to the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Range uses ends only; MAD averages every absolute deviation.","explain":"Mean = 16/3. Absolute deviations differ from 7. Range ≠ MAD in general."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"{n} identical absolute deviations of {d} each. What is the MAD?","answer":"d","hint":"Average of n copies of d is d.","explain":"MAD = ({n}×{d})/{n} = {d}.","params":{"n":[3,8],"d":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The MAD is…","choices":["the average of the absolute deviations from the mean","the largest value minus the smallest","another name for the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: average of |value − mean|, not the range.","explain":"MAD = mean of |each value − mean|. Range uses only the extremes; the mean is the centre, not the spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Four absolute deviations from the mean are {a}, {a}, {a} and {a}. What is the MAD?","answer":"a","hint":"Average four equal absolute deviations — the average equals each one.","explain":"MAD = ({a}+{a}+{a}+{a})/4 = {a}. Equal absolute deviations average to that same value.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Set A: 5, 5, 5. Set B: 1, 5, 9. Both have mean 5. Which has the larger MAD?","choices":["Set B","Set A","They must have the same MAD because the means match"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same centre can hide different typical distances from that centre.","explain":"Set A has MAD 0. Set B's values sit farther from 5, so MAD is larger. Same mean ≠ same MAD."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Values {a}, {a}, {a}. Absolute deviations from the mean are all 0. What is the MAD?","answer":"0","hint":"If every value equals the mean, every absolute deviation is 0.","explain":"All values are {a}, so mean = {a} and MAD = 0. Zero MAD means no spread about the mean.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Four absolute deviations from the mean are {a}, {a}, {b}, {b}. What is the MAD?","answer":"(a+a+b+b)/4","hint":"MAD is the average of the absolute deviations.","explain":"MAD = ({a}+{a}+{b}+{b})/4 = {=(a+a+b+b)/4}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["First find the mean of your data. Then for each value, find how far it is from that mean — ignore whether it was above or below. That distance is the absolute deviation.","Add those absolute deviations and divide by how many values you have. The result is the mean absolute deviation, or MAD. It tells you the typical distance from the mean.","Two data sets can share the same mean but have different MAD. Scores 4, 5, 5, 6 and scores 1, 5, 5, 9 both have mean 5, but the second set is more spread out so its MAD is larger.","MAD is not the range (largest minus smallest) and not the mean itself. Range uses only the ends; MAD uses every value's distance from the centre."],"adult":["CCSS 6.SP.5c: use centre and spread (including MAD) with the shape of the data. England KS3: find and read averages and spread. Not the same as Statistical questions and variability (flagspread) or Dot plots and histograms (flaghist).","Words: mean, absolute deviation, MAD, spread. Common mistakes: calling MAD the range; thinking MAD is the mean; forgetting absolute value so signed gaps cancel; averaging only the biggest gap.","In the lab your child finds the mean, marks each |x − mean|, averages them into the MAD, compares two same-mean sets, spots mean/range mix-ups, and practises the steps."]},"familyTip":"With five dinner times or phone-battery percentages, find the mean together, then the absolute distance of each number from that mean, then average those distances — that average is the MAD.","keyIdea":"The mean absolute deviation (MAD) is the average of how far each value sits from the mean: for each value find |value − mean|, then average those absolute deviations. Same mean can hide very different MAD; MAD measures typical distance from the centre, not the range and not the mean itself.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"MAD idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True MAD idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["MAD averages |value − mean|",0],["Two sets can share a mean but have different MAD",0],["MAD is the same as the range",1],["MAD is just another name for the mean",1],["Absolute deviation drops the sign of the distance",0],["A bigger MAD means values sit farther from the mean on average",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmad","caption":"Flagship lab: find the mean, mark absolute deviations, average them into MAD, compare two same-mean sets, catch mean/range mix-ups, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Cqm8iy48UI":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A train leaves at 14:05. What is that in 12-hour time?","choices":["2:05 pm","4:05 pm","14:05 am"],"answer":0,"hint":"For times after 12:00, take away 12 hours.","explain":"14 − 12 = 2, so 14:05 is 2:05 in the afternoon, written 2:05 pm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A train leaves at 11:{a} and arrives at 12:{b}. How many minutes is the trip?","answer":"60-a+b","hint":"Jump to 12:00 first, then count on the rest.","explain":"11:{a} to 12:00 is {=60-a} minutes, and 12:00 to 12:{b} is {b} minutes, so the trip is {=60-a+b} minutes.","params":{"a":[40,58],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bus 1 leaves at 08:45 and arrives at 09:16. Sam says the trip is 71 minutes because 916 − 845 = 71. What is wrong?","choices":["an hour has 60 minutes, so the trip is 31 minutes","nothing, it is 71 minutes","he should add the times"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count on from 08:45 to 09:00, then to 09:16.","explain":"08:45 to 09:00 is 15 minutes and 09:00 to 09:16 is 16 minutes, so 31 minutes. Taking 845 from 916 treats an hour as 100 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"In a two-way table, the Netball column has {a} in Year 5 and {b} in Year 6. What is the Netball total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"A column total adds up all the cells above it.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The total at the bottom of a column is the sum of that column.","params":{"a":[4,15],"b":[4,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You reach the Library at 09:40. The Library row says 08:58, 09:33, 10:18, 11:52. When is the next bus?","choices":["10:18","09:33","11:52"],"answer":0,"hint":"The next bus is the first time after 09:40.","explain":"08:58 and 09:33 have already gone. The first time after 09:40 is 10:18."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You must be at the Library by 09:40. Bus 1 gets there at 08:58, Bus 2 at 09:33 and Bus 3 at 10:18. Which is the latest bus you can catch?","choices":["Bus 2","Bus 1","Bus 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Latest means the last one that still gets there in time.","explain":"Bus 1 and Bus 2 both arrive before 09:40, but Bus 2 is later, so it is the latest one that works. Bus 3 arrives at 10:18, too late."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A two-way table has a Total column with {a} for Year 5 and {b} for Year 6. What is the grand total in the bottom corner?","answer":"a+b","hint":"The grand total adds up the Total column. The Total row gives the same number.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Adding the Total row would give {=a+b} too, which is a good check.","params":{"a":[20,40],"b":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a timetable, Bus 3 reaches the Library at 10:18 and Market Square at 10:27. When does it reach Market Square?","choices":["10:27","10:18","10:05"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find Bus 3's column, then the Market Square row.","explain":"10:27 is in the Market Square row. 10:18 is the Library row, and 10:05 is when it leaves the first stop."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a bus timetable, each column is one bus and each row is one stop. How do you find when Bus 3 reaches the Station?","choices":["go down Bus 3's column to the Station row","go along the Station row and pick the first time","go down Bus 3's column and pick the last time you see"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need the cell that is in both Bus 3's column and the Station row.","explain":"The answer is in the cell where Bus 3's column meets the Station row. The first time in the Station row is a different bus."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A bus leaves at 9:{a} and arrives at 10:{b}. How many minutes does the trip take?","answer":"60-a+b","hint":"Count on from 9:{a} to 10:00 first. Then count on to 10:{b}.","explain":"From 9:{a} to 10:00 is {=60-a} minutes. From 10:00 to 10:{b} is {b} minutes. {=60-a} + {b} = {=60-a+b} minutes.","params":{"a":[35,55],"b":[10,25]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a two-way table, the Year 6 row has {a} for football, {b} for swimming and a gap for netball. The row total is {t}. What number goes in the gap?","answer":"t-a-b","hint":"The row total is all the cells in the row added up. Take the cells you know away from it.","explain":"{t} − {a} − {b} = {=t-a-b}. Check: {a} + {b} + {=t-a-b} = {t}.","params":{"a":[5,15],"b":[5,15],"t":[40,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A timetable shows a dash (—) for Bus 2 at Market Square. What does it mean?","choices":["Bus 2 does not stop at Market Square","Bus 2 waits at Market Square","the time was left out by mistake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Timetables use a dash on purpose.","explain":"A dash means the bus does not stop there. You need to find the next bus that has a time in that row."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You must be at the Station by 10:45. Bus 3 gets there at 10:36 and Bus 4 gets there at 12:14. Which bus should you catch?","choices":["Bus 3","Bus 4","either one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which bus arrives before 10:45?","explain":"Bus 3 arrives at 10:36, before 10:45. Bus 4 arrives at 12:14, which is too late."}],"explain":{"kid":["A table puts facts in rows and columns. Rows go across. Columns go down. Each box, or cell, sits in one row and one column.","In a bus timetable, each column is one bus. Each row is one stop. To find when a bus gets to a stop, go down that bus's column. Stop at the row for your stop. Read the time in that cell. A dash (—) means the bus does not stop there.","To find how long a trip takes, count on. A bus leaves at 08:45 and gets in at 09:16. Count 15 minutes to 09:00. Then count 16 more. That is 31 minutes. Do not take 845 from 916. An hour has 60 minutes, not 100.","A two-way table sorts things two ways at once, like year group and favourite sport. Each total adds up its own row or column. If a cell is missing, take the other cells away from the total."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. In the National Curriculum in England, Year 4 statistics asks children to interpret and present data in tables, and Year 5 asks them to complete, read and interpret information in tables, including timetables. Timetables also practise elapsed time across the hour (Year 4 measurement; CCSS 3.MD.1). This leads into pictograms and tally charts, using several sources, and two-way tables with relative frequencies.","The skill is locating a cell from two headings and then using it: reading a time, choosing a bus by its arrival time, or working out a duration. Timetables are usually written in the 24-hour clock (14:05 is 2:05 pm), and a dash or blank means a service does not call at that stop.","Common mistakes: reading the right column but the wrong row, such as the Library time for Market Square. Choosing a bus by when it leaves when the question gives a time to arrive by. Taking times away as if they were ordinary numbers: 09:16 − 08:45 gives 71, not 31. In two-way tables, children often add the total into the row instead of taking the other cells away from it.","In the lab your child taps cells in a bus timetable with four stops to answer questions. They work around the dash and drag a marker along a time line to count on past an o'clock. They find the latest bus that still arrives in time. Then they fill the gaps in a two-way table about favourite sports and check that the row and column totals agree."]},"familyTip":"Next time you take a bus or train, or look one up online, read the timetable together. Ask: which column is our bus? Which row is our stop? If we must be there by a set time, which is the latest one we can catch, and how long will the trip take?","keyIdea":"A table sorts information into rows and columns. In a bus timetable each column is one bus and each row is one stop, so you go down the bus's column to the stop's row and read the time. A dash means the bus does not stop there. To find how long a trip takes, count on to the next o'clock and then add the rest, because an hour has 60 minutes, not 100. In a two-way table, each total adds up its own row or column, so you can work out a missing number.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each question need one column (one bus) or one row (one stop)? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["read down one bus's column","read along one stop's row"],"items":[["every stop Bus 3 makes",0],["every bus that leaves Park Road",1],["when Bus 1 reaches each stop",0],["the first bus at the Library",1],["how long Bus 2 takes from start to end",0],["the last bus at the Station",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtimetab","caption":"Flagship lab: read a bus timetable by column and row, meet the dash, count on to time a trip, find the latest bus, and fill a two-way table.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fmm-P17Vka":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word means the value that comes up most often?","choices":["mode","median","range"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most often means the highest frequency.","explain":"The mode is the most common value. The median is the middle value in order, and the range is the largest minus the smallest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Shoe sizes 2, 5, 5, 6, 8. One child with size 13 joins. Which measure changes the most?","choices":["the mean","the median","the mode"],"answer":0,"hint":"An outlier pulls the total up a lot. Which measure uses the total?","explain":"Size 13 is an outlier. The mean uses the total, so it rises a lot (from 5.2 to about 6.5). The median moves from 5 to 5.5, and the mode stays 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Find the median of {=a+4}, {a}, {=a+8}, {=a+2}, {=a+6}.","answer":"a+4","hint":"Order the five numbers from smallest to largest, then take the third one.","explain":"In order: {a}, {=a+2}, {=a+4}, {=a+6}, {=a+8}. The middle (third) value is {=a+4}.","params":{"a":[1,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Find the mean of {a}, {=a+2}, {=a+4} and {=a+6}.","answer":"a+3","hint":"Add the four numbers, then divide the total by 4.","explain":"Total {=4*a+12} ÷ 4 = {=a+3}. The mean is {=a+3}.","params":{"a":[1,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Times in a race: 12 s, 15 s, 14 s, 20 s. What is the range?","choices":["8 s","20 s","14.5 s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Range = largest time − smallest time.","explain":"Largest 20 s − smallest 12 s = 8 s. 20 s is only the largest value, and 14.5 s is the median."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ice cream sales and sunburn cases both rise in summer. A scatter graph shows positive correlation. What can you say?","choices":["they rise together, but ice cream does not cause sunburn","ice cream causes sunburn","there is no correlation"],"answer":0,"hint":"Correlation describes a pattern. Is there a third thing, like sunny weather, behind both?","explain":"Both rise together, which is positive correlation. Sunny weather drives both. Correlation alone never proves cause."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Scores 4, 9, 6, 6, 10. Which statement is true?","choices":["mode 6, median 6, range 6","mode 6, median 6, range 10","mode 2, median 6, range 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mode: most common value. Median: middle once in order. Range: largest minus smallest.","explain":"In order: 4, 6, 6, 9, 10. The mode is 6 (it comes up twice), the median is 6, and the range is 10 − 4 = 6. The 2 is the frequency of 6, not the mode."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is an outlier?","choices":["a value far from the rest of the data","the largest value in any set","the value that comes up most often"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of one dot sitting a long way from the others.","explain":"An outlier is far from the rest. Every set has a largest value, but that is only an outlier if it is far away. The most common value is the mode."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Favourite pets: dog 6 votes, cat 8 votes, fish 3 votes. What is the mode?","choices":["cat","8","dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mode is the most common answer. Which pet has the highest frequency?","explain":"Cat has the highest frequency (8 votes), so the mode is cat. The number 8 is the frequency, not the mode itself."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Scores 7, 3, 9, 1, 5. What is the median?","choices":["5","9","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put the scores in order from smallest to largest first, then take the middle one.","explain":"In order: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. The middle value is 5. The 9 sits in the middle of the list as written, but that list was not in order."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Heights of three plants: {a} cm, {=a+2} cm and {=a+b} cm. What is the range in cm?","answer":"b","hint":"Range = largest value − smallest value. Which plant is tallest, and which is shortest?","explain":"Largest {=a+b} − smallest {a} = {b}. So the range is {b} cm.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[4,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Three children read {a}, {b} and {c} books. What is the mean number of books?","answer":"(a+b+c)/3","hint":"Add the three numbers to get the total, then share the total equally between 3 children.","explain":"Total {=a+b+c} ÷ 3 = {=(a+b+c)/3}. The mean is {=(a+b+c)/3} books.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[2,8],"c":[3,12]},"where":"(a+b+c)%3==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"On a scatter graph of hours of practice and spelling score, the dots rise from left to right. This shows…","choices":["positive correlation","negative correlation","no correlation"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do both values go up together, or does one go down as the other goes up?","explain":"Both values go up together, so it is positive correlation. Negative would mean the dots fall from left to right."}],"explain":{"kid":["Data is a set of facts, often numbers. Frequency is how many times a value comes up. The mode is the value that comes up most often. If two values tie, there are two modes.","The median is the middle value. First put the numbers in order, from small to big. Then find the one in the middle. If there are two in the middle, the median is halfway between them.","The mean is a fair share. Add up all the values to get the total. Then divide by how many values there are. The range is the biggest value take away the smallest. It shows how spread out the data is.","An outlier is a value far from the rest. It can pull the mean a long way, but the median hardly moves. A scatter graph shows pairs of values as dots. If both go up together, that is a positive pattern. If one goes up as the other goes down, it is negative. If the dots are all over, there is no pattern. The word for this pattern is correlation."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. In the National Curriculum in England, Year 6 statistics asks pupils to calculate and interpret the mean as an average. Mode, median and range are common in Key Stage 2 practice. Key Stage 3 adds measures of spread, outliers, and scatter graphs for two variables. This lesson teaches the words so a child can say what each measure describes, not only work it out.","Mode: the most frequent value or category. A set can have two modes, or none if every value occurs once. Median: the middle of the ordered data; with an even number of values, it is the mean of the two middle values. Mean: the sum divided by the number of values. Range: largest minus smallest, a measure of spread rather than an average. An outlier is a value far from the rest; it moves the mean and the range a lot and the median very little. Correlation describes the trend on a scatter graph: positive, negative or none.","Common mistakes: giving the highest frequency (the count) as the mode, instead of the value it belongs to. Finding the middle of a list that has not been put in order. Mixing up mean and median. Giving the largest value as the range. Reading a correlation as proof that one thing causes the other.","In the lab your child adds votes until grape becomes the mode. They swap five score cards into order to find the median. They move cubes between towers of 2, 4 and 9 until all are 5, the mean. They drag one quiz score out to make an outlier while the median stays put. Last, they label three scatter graphs as positive, negative or no correlation."]},"familyTip":"Ask everyone at home how many hours they slept last night. Write the numbers down. Together, find the mode (most common), put them in order to find the median, add and share to find the mean, and take the smallest from the largest for the range. Then ask: what would happen to each if a baby who slept 16 hours joined in?","keyIdea":"Each statistics word names one job. Frequency counts how often a value comes up. The mode is the most common value. The median is the middle value once the data is in order. The mean is the total shared equally. The range is the largest value minus the smallest. An outlier is far from the rest. A scatter graph shows pairs of values, and correlation names their pattern.","visual":{"type":"statlab","caption":"Drag the dots. Watch the mean (dashed line), the median (triangle), the mode and the range change.","opts":{"vals":[2,4,5,7,7,9,14],"max":20,"target":8}},"lab":{"type":"flagstatvoc","caption":"Flagship lab: vote to change the mode, swap score cards to find the median, level cube towers to find the mean, drag an outlier, and label three scatter graphs.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Cg8VPguS_V":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A family scored 6, 8, 7, 5 and 9 in five rounds of a board game. What is the mean score?","answer":7,"hint":"Add the five scores, then divide by the number of rounds.","explain":"6 + 8 + 7 + 5 + 9 = 35, and 35 ÷ 5 = 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the mean of 2, 0 and 4?","choices":["2","3","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many values are there? Does the 0 count?","explain":"The total is 6, and there are 3 values, including the 0. 6 ÷ 3 = 2. Leaving out the 0 and dividing by 2 gives 3, which is wrong."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Find the mean of {a}, {=a+2} and {=a+4}.","answer":"a+2","hint":"Add the three numbers, then divide by 3. Or even them out: take 2 from the biggest and give it to the smallest.","explain":"The total is 3 × {a} + 6 = {=3*a+6}, and {=3*a+6} ÷ 3 = {=a+2}. Evening out the three numbers gives the same answer.","params":{"a":[3,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Scores 6, 8, 7, 5 and 9 have a mean of 7. The 9 is changed to 24. What happens to the mean?","choices":["It goes up by 3, to 10","It goes up by 15, to 22","It stays at 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"The total goes up by 15. How is that shared between 5 scores?","explain":"The new total is 50, and 50 ÷ 5 = 10. The extra 15 is shared between all 5 scores, so the mean only rises by 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Two scores have a mean of {m}. One of them is {a}. What is the other score?","answer":"2*m-a","hint":"Two scores with a mean of {m} must add up to {m} × 2.","explain":"The total is {m} × 2 = {=2*m}. The other score is {=2*m} − {a} = {=2*m-a}.","params":{"m":[10,20],"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Find the mean of 4, 6, 7 and 9.","choices":["6.5","5.2","26"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the values carefully before you divide.","explain":"The total is 26 and there are 4 values, so the mean is 26 ÷ 4 = 6.5. Dividing by 5 gives 5.2, and 26 is only the total."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Three scores have a mean of 10. Two of them are 8 and 9. What is the third score?","choices":["13","10","17"],"answer":0,"hint":"First find the total the three scores must make.","explain":"The total must be 10 × 3 = 30. The two you know make 17, so the third is 30 − 17 = 13. The missing value does not have to equal the mean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Team A (4 players) scored a mean of 12 goals each. Team B (6 players) scored a mean of 9 goals each. Which team scored more goals in total?","choices":["Team B, with 54 goals","Team A, because its mean is higher","They scored the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn each mean back into a total: mean × number of players.","explain":"Team A scored 12 × 4 = 48 goals and Team B scored 9 × 6 = 54. A higher mean does not mean a higher total when the groups are different sizes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Six children have heights of 128, 130, 131, 133, 134 and 170 cm. Why is the mean a poor way to describe a typical height here?","choices":["The 170 cm value pulls the mean above most of the heights","The mean can't be used for heights","There are too few heights to add up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out the mean, then compare it with the heights.","explain":"The total is 826, and 826 ÷ 6 is about 137.7 cm, which is taller than five of the six children. One extreme value pulls the mean a long way."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How do you find the mean of a set of numbers?","choices":["Add them all, then divide by how many numbers there are","Pick the number in the middle","Take the smallest from the biggest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of tipping every tower onto one pile, then sharing the pile out.","explain":"The mean is the total shared equally: add all the values, then divide by how many values there are. The middle number is the median, and biggest minus smallest is the range."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Four quiz scores have a mean of {m}. What is the total of the four scores?","answer":"4*m","hint":"Mean = total ÷ 4, so the total is the mean times 4.","explain":"Total = mean × number of values = {m} × 4 = {=4*m}.","params":{"m":[5,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Find the mean of 72, 85, 90, 68 and 95.","answer":82,"hint":"Add the five scores first, then divide the total by 5.","explain":"72 + 85 + 90 + 68 + 95 = 410, and 410 ÷ 5 = 82. The mean is 82, even though nobody scored exactly 82."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Five scores have a mean of {m}. Four of them add up to {t}. What is the fifth score?","answer":"5*m-t","hint":"Work out the total the five scores must make, then take away the four you know.","explain":"The total must be {m} × 5 = {=5*m}. Then {=5*m} − {t} = {=5*m-t}, so the fifth score is {=5*m-t}.","params":{"m":[6,10],"t":[16,24]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Mia says the mean of 3, 5, 6 and 8 can't be 5.5 because nobody scored 5.5. Is she right?","choices":["No: the mean is a fair share and can be a value nobody got","Yes: the mean is always one of the values","Yes: the mean must be a whole number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Level the towers in your head. Where do the 2 spare cubes go?","explain":"3 + 5 + 6 + 8 = 22, and 22 ÷ 4 = 5.5. Sharing the total equally gives 5.5 each, so the mean can be a decimal that is not in the data."}],"explain":{"kid":["An average is one number that sums up a set of numbers. The mean is the average you get by sharing. Add up all the values to get the total. Then divide the total by how many values there are. For the scores 6, 8, 7, 5 and 9, the total is 35. There are 5 scores, and 35 ÷ 5 = 7, so the mean is 7.","Think of each score as a tower of cubes. Move cubes from the tall towers to the short ones until they are all level. No cubes are added or lost, so the total stays the same. The level they end up at is the mean.","The mean does not have to be one of the values. The mean of 3, 5, 6 and 8 is 22 ÷ 4 = 5.5, even though nobody scored 5.5. Every value counts, even a 0. One very big score pulls the mean up, but only by its share. If one score of 9 becomes 24, the total goes up by 15, and 15 ÷ 5 = 3, so the mean goes up by 3.","You can work backwards. If 5 rounds have a mean of 8, the total must be 8 × 5 = 40. If four of the scores add up to 34, the last one is 40 − 34 = 6."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11 (NC Year 6 statistics: calculate and interpret the mean as an average; CCSS 6.SP.A.3 and 6.SP.B.5c). The mean is the total divided by the number of values. It is often explained as a fair share, or as the level reached when towers of cubes are evened out; the lab uses both pictures.","Common mistakes: stopping at the total and not dividing; dividing by the wrong count, often by leaving out a value of 0; giving the middle value (the median) or the most common value (the mode) when the mean is asked for; believing the mean must be a whole number or one of the data values; and thinking a missing value must equal the mean.","Interpreting the mean matters as much as calculating it. Because every value is used, one extreme value (an outlier) can pull the mean a long way from most of the data, which is when the median may describe the set better. The mean also lets you compare groups of different sizes, such as two teams with different numbers of players.","In the lab your child levels five score towers, tips them into one pile and shares it out, levels 3, 5, 6 and 8 and cuts the spare cubes in half to reach 5.5, stretches one score to 24 and watches the mean rise by only 3, then sets a missing score so the mean is exactly 8."]},"familyTip":"After a board game or a few rounds of a quiz, write down each person's scores. Stack coins or Lego bricks to match the scores, then move bricks from the tall stacks to the short ones until they are level. Check it with a sum: add the scores and divide by how many rounds there were.","keyIdea":"The mean is one kind of average. To find it, add up all the values to get the total, then divide by how many values there are: mean = total ÷ number of values. It is the level every value would have if the total were shared out equally. The mean does not have to be one of the values, and it can be a decimal. Every value counts, so one very big or very small value moves the mean. If you know the mean, the total is mean × number of values, which lets you find a missing value.","visual":{"type":"share","caption":"The mean shares the total out fairly. Deal 35 points equally between 5 rounds: each round gets 7.","opts":{"total":35,"groups":5}},"lab":{"type":"flagmean","caption":"Flagship lab: level towers of cubes, pile them up and share them out, split spare cubes into halves, stretch one score and find a missing one.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VhBH8wrFC6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In a picture graph, one picture stands for one child. Banana has 3 pictures. How many children chose banana?","choices":["3","6","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"One picture is one child. Count the pictures.","explain":"3 pictures, one child each, so 3 children chose banana."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Bars show apple {a}, pear {b} and plum {c}. How many votes in all?","answer":"a+b+c","hint":"In all means add every bar.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {c} = {=a+b+c} votes in all.","params":{"a":[3,7],"b":[1,5],"c":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam draws a bar for 5 votes, but it stops on the line marked 6. What went wrong?","choices":["He counted the 0 line as 1","Nothing, it is right","He used the wrong colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does every bar start?","explain":"Bars start at 0. If you count the 0 line as 1, the bar ends one line too high."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Cat has 7 votes and fish has 2. Mia says cat has 9 more. What did she do?","choices":["She added instead of finding the gap","She read the cat bar wrong","Nothing, 9 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many more is a difference, not a total.","explain":"7 + 2 = 9 is the total. How many more is the gap: 7 − 2 = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"The dog bar is at {a} and the rabbit bar is at {b}. How many fewer chose rabbit?","answer":"a-b","hint":"How many fewer is the gap between the tops too.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} fewer chose rabbit.","params":{"a":[6,8],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What should a bar graph have at the top?","choices":["a title saying what it shows","the biggest number","nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"How would someone know what the graph is about?","explain":"A title, like 'Our favourite fruit', tells the reader what was counted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Cat 7, Dog 5, Fish 2, Rabbit 4. Which pet got 3 fewer votes than cat?","choices":["Rabbit","Dog","Fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take 3 from cat's 7, then find the bar at that number.","explain":"7 − 3 = 4, and the rabbit bar stops at 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which question needs you to add two bars?","choices":["How many chose dog or rabbit?","How many more chose dog than rabbit?","Which pet has the tallest bar?"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Or' and 'together' put groups together.","explain":"Dog or rabbit puts two groups together, so add the bars. How many more needs the gap."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A bar graph shows {a} votes for cats and {b} votes for dogs. How many more voted for cats?","answer":"a-b","hint":"How many more is the gap between the bar tops. Take the smaller from the bigger.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. The cat bar is {=a-b} squares taller.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A bar graph shows {a} children chose red and {b} chose blue. How many chose red or blue?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Red or blue puts the two groups together. Add the bars.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} children chose red or blue.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The pear bar starts at 0 and stops on the line marked 4. How many votes for pear?","choices":["4","5","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the number on the line at the top of the bar.","explain":"The bar stops on the line marked 4, so pear has 4 votes. Do not count the 0 line."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which label goes up the side axis of a bar graph about favourite fruit?","choices":["Number of children","Fruit","Apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"The side axis has the numbers on it.","explain":"The side axis counts, so its label is 'Number of children'. The fruit names go along the bottom."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{t} children voted. {a} chose grape. How many did not choose grape?","answer":"t-a","hint":"Take apart: take the grape bar away from the total.","explain":"{t} − {a} = {=t-a} children did not choose grape.","params":{"t":[15,20],"a":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A picture graph shows a count with pictures. One picture stands for one vote. Count the pictures to read it.","A bar graph uses bars. Each bar starts at 0. It stops on the line with its number. Read the number at the top of the bar.","Every graph needs a title on top. The side axis shows the numbers. The bottom axis names the groups, like apple or pear.","To find how many in two groups, add the bars. To find how many more, look at the gap between the two tops. Grape 6 and pear 2: the gap is 4."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This is CCSS 2.MD.10: draw a picture graph and a bar graph with a single-unit scale for up to four categories, and solve put-together, take-apart and compare problems using a bar graph. In England, Year 2 statistics asks children to interpret and construct simple pictograms, tally charts, block diagrams and tables. Key words: data, category, picture graph, bar graph, scale, axis, title, label, total, difference.","Common mistakes: counting the 0 line as 1, so every bar ends one too high; reading the space under a line instead of the line; adding the two bars when the question asks how many more; and leaving off the title or axis labels, so nobody knows what was counted.","Single-unit scale means each step on the side axis is worth 1. Later, in Year 3 and grade 3, the scale goes up in 2s, 5s or 10s, which is why reading the number on the line, not counting squares, matters now.","In the lab your child adds one picture per vote, drags bars to the right line, places the title and axis labels, taps the bars a question needs, and slides a level line to see a difference."]},"familyTip":"Ask everyone at home to vote for a pudding. Draw a bar graph on squared paper with one square for each vote, then take turns asking questions: 'How many more chose ice cream than jelly?' 'How many chose fruit or yogurt?' Check each answer by counting squares.","keyIdea":"A picture graph uses one picture for each thing counted. A bar graph uses bars instead. Each bar starts at 0 and stops on the line that matches its number, so you read a bar by the number at its top. Give the graph a title and label both axes: the side axis counts, the bottom axis names the groups. To put together, add bars. To take apart, take a bar from the total. To compare, find the gap between two bar tops: that is how many more or fewer.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which kind of question is it? Drag each to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Add the bars","Find the gap between bars"],"items":[["How many chose cat or dog?",0],["How many more chose cat than fish?",1],["How many children voted in all?",0],["How many fewer chose fish than dog?",1],["How many chose red and blue together?",0],["How much taller is the apple bar than the pear bar?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbgraph","caption":"Flagship lab: build a picture graph, drag bars to a 0 to 8 scale, label the axes, then add, take apart and compare bars.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VRieg2xgD3":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A factory makes {=50*k} phones a day. Quality control wants a systematic sample of 50. What is the sampling interval?","answer":"k","hint":"Divide the number of phones by the sample size.","explain":"{=50*k} ÷ 50 = {k}, so test every {k}th phone after a random start.","params":{"k":[3,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A club has 300 members: {=30*a} juniors and the rest seniors. How many juniors in a stratified sample of 30?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Find the juniors’ share of the club, then take that share of 30.","explain":"{=30*a} ÷ 300 × 30 = {=3*a} juniors.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What makes a sample random?","choices":["Every member has a known, equal chance of being chosen","The person choosing picks a good mix","It is large"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who decides who is picked.","explain":"In a random sample chance decides, and every member has the same chance. A big or hand-picked sample can still be biased."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A random number repeats. What do you do in a simple random sample?","choices":["Skip it and use the next number","Count that person twice","Start again from the beginning"],"answer":0,"hint":"Should one person be in the sample twice?","explain":"Skip the repeat so each person appears at most once, and carry on until the sample is full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A school has 600 girls and 400 boys. For a stratified sample of 50, which split is right?","choices":["30 girls, 20 boys","25 girls, 25 boys","60 girls, 40 boys"],"answer":0,"hint":"Girls are 600 of 1000 students.","explain":"600 ÷ 1000 × 50 = 30 girls and 400 ÷ 1000 × 50 = 20 boys."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"For a systematic sample with k = 8, where should the start be?","choices":["A random number from 1 to 8","Always 1","Always 8"],"answer":0,"hint":"The start should be left to chance too.","explain":"Choosing the start at random from 1 to 8 gives every member the same 1 in 8 chance."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A sampling frame lists {=25*n} households. You want a sample of 25. Using a systematic sample, how many households are skipped between each one picked?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Find the interval first. You pick one in each block of that size.","explain":"The interval is {=25*n} ÷ 25 = {n}, so {=n-1} households are skipped between picks.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why might a researcher choose stratified sampling?","choices":["To make sure each group is represented in proportion","Because it needs no random choice","Because it is the quickest to set up"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does stratifying guarantee?","explain":"Stratifying fixes each group’s share so every group is represented in proportion. Random choice is still used inside each group."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Does a random sample always match the population exactly?","choices":["No, results still vary by chance","Yes, random means exact","Yes, if it is systematic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two random samples from the same population can give different results.","explain":"Random selection removes bias in who is chosen, but any sample still varies by chance. Bigger samples vary less."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A list has {=20*k} names. You want a systematic sample of 20. What is the sampling interval k?","answer":"k","hint":"Interval = population size ÷ sample size.","explain":"{=20*k} ÷ 20 = {k}, so take every {k}th name after a random start from 1 to {k}.","params":{"k":[4,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A school has 800 students. Year 7 has {=40*y} students. How many Year 7 students should be in a stratified sample of 40?","answer":"2*y","hint":"Year 7’s share of the school, times the sample size: (size ÷ 800) × 40.","explain":"{=40*y} ÷ 800 × 40 = {=2*y} Year 7 students.","params":{"y":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Students are numbered 01 to 60. The random number table gives 72. What should you do?","choices":["Skip 72 and read the next pair","Use student 60 instead","Use student 12, since 72 − 60 = 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would swapping in another number give some students an extra chance?","explain":"There is no student 72, so skip it. Swapping in another student would give that student more chances than the rest."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Every 10th flat in a block is a large flat on the corner. Is a systematic sample with k = 10 a good idea?","choices":["No, it could pick only corner flats or none","Yes, systematic samples are always fair","Yes, if you start at flat 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the interval with the pattern in the list.","explain":"The list repeats every 10, the same as k, so the sample could be all corner flats or none. Use a simple random sample."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is NOT a random sample?","choices":["The first 30 people through the door","30 names drawn from a hat of everyone","30 IDs chosen by a random number generator"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does everyone have the same chance of being first through the door?","explain":"The first 30 through the door is an opportunity (convenience) sample. People who arrive early are more likely to be picked."}],"explain":{"kid":["A sample is a small group chosen to stand for a bigger group, the population. A sample is only useful if it is fair. Random choice makes it fair: every member has the same chance of being picked.","Simple random sample: give everyone a number, then use random numbers to choose. If a number comes up twice, skip it. If a number is not on the list, skip that too.","Systematic sample: to choose 6 from a list of 60, take every 10th person, because 60 ÷ 6 = 10. Pick the first one at random from 1 to 10. Watch out: if the list has a pattern that repeats every 10, you might pick only one kind of person.","Stratified sample: split the population into groups, like year groups. Give each group a share of the sample that matches its size. A year with 240 of 800 students gets 240 ÷ 800 × 40 = 12 of a sample of 40. Then pick at random inside each group."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. GCSE Mathematics and GCSE Statistics (AQA, Edexcel, OCR) ask students to describe and carry out simple random, systematic and stratified sampling. They must also work out a stratified sample in proportion. CCSS 7.SP.1 shows random sampling as the way to get a fair sample. Key terms: population, sampling frame, sample, simple random sample, systematic sample, sampling interval, stratum (strata), proportional allocation, bias.","Exact meanings: in a simple random sample, every possible sample of size n is equally likely. So each member also has the same chance. A systematic sample with a random start gives each member a 1 in k chance, but not every set of members can be picked. So it is not a simple random sample. It goes wrong when the list has a pattern with the same gap as k. A stratified sample fixes how many come from each group, usually in proportion, then picks at random inside each group. That makes sure every group is there.","Common mistakes: swapping an out-of-range number for a nearby one, which gives some people extra chances; keeping repeats; using n or N × n instead of N ÷ n for the gap; always starting at 1 instead of at random; giving each group the same number whatever its size; rounding the groups so the total is no longer the sample size; and calling a sample of whoever is nearby random. Random choice removes bias in who is picked. It does not make a sample match the population exactly.","In the lab you accept or skip eight random number pairs for a list of 50. You set the gap and start for a sample of 6 from 60, then find the start that picks only corner houses on a street where every 10th house is a corner house. You set the numbers for a stratified sample from a school of 800, and match situations to methods. Every wrong move names the slip."]},"familyTip":"Take a class register or a list of 30 names. Roll two dice, or use a phone’s random number tool, to pick 5 names, and talk about what to do with a repeat or a number over 30. Then try every 6th name from a random start. Finally ask: if the class is 18 girls and 12 boys, how many of each should a stratified sample of 10 have? (6 and 4.)","keyIdea":"A random sample gives every member of the population a known, equal chance of being picked, so who ends up in the sample is not decided by convenience or by the person choosing. In a simple random sample you number everyone and use random numbers, skipping repeats and numbers not on the list. In a systematic sample you take every kth member, where k = population ÷ sample size, after a random start from 1 to k; it fails if the list repeats with the same gap. In a stratified sample you split the population into groups and sample each in proportion: (group size ÷ population) × sample size, then choose at random within each group.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sampling method is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Simple random","Systematic","Stratified"],"items":[["Raffle tickets drawn from a drum",0],["Every 50th phone off the line, random start",1],["Same share of each shift as the workforce",2],["Random number generator picks 40 IDs",0],["Every 10th visitor from a random first visitor",1],["Each region sampled in proportion to its population",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrsamp","caption":"Flagship lab: random number table, systematic interval and start, a hidden pattern, a stratified sample, and matching methods.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QGZgv63WxC":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is bias in a survey?","choices":["A fault in the method that pushes results the same way each time","Random luck in who gets picked","Any result you did not expect"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about whether repeating the survey would fix it.","explain":"Bias is systematic: repeat the survey the same way and it leans the same way again. Random luck varies from sample to sample."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"An online poll on a cycling club website asks if the town needs more cycle lanes. Why might it be biased?","choices":["Most people who see and answer it are cyclists","The question is too short","Online polls always have too few replies"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who visits that website, and who chooses to answer?","explain":"It is a self-selected sample from a cycling site, so cyclists are over-represented and the yes share comes out too high."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"In a town, 40% of people are teens. A sample of 50 at a skate shop has {t} teens. How many more teens is that than a representative sample of 50 would have?","answer":"t-20","hint":"First find 40% of 50, the number of teens a fair sample would have.","explain":"A representative sample of 50 would have 40% teens, which is 20. {t} − 20 = {=t-20} extra teens.","params":{"t":[25,48]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a leading question?","choices":["Wouldn't you agree that homework is a waste of time?","How many hours of homework do you do a week?","Do you do homework at the weekend?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one tells you the answer it hopes for?","explain":"'Wouldn't you agree…' pushes people towards yes. The other two ask for facts without hinting at an answer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Forms went to a random sample, but only 20% of teens and 60% of adults replied. What kind of bias is this?","choices":["Non-response bias","A leading question","Sampling bias in who was sent forms"],"answer":0,"hint":"The forms went to a fair sample. What went wrong after that?","explain":"The sample was random, but the replies over-represent adults. That is non-response bias."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which change fixes non-response bias best?","choices":["Follow up with reminders until all groups reply at similar rates","Send forms to ten times as many people","Ask a shorter question"],"answer":0,"hint":"The problem is who replies, not how many forms are sent.","explain":"Follow-ups raise the reply rate of the groups that were missing. More forms keep the same mix of replies."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"In a model, a neutral question gets {y}% yes. A leading version adds 15 percentage points. What percentage say yes to the leading version?","answer":"y+15","hint":"Add the 15 points to the neutral result.","explain":"{y}% plus 15 points is {=y+15}%. The people did not change; only the wording did.","params":{"y":[30,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is a random sample from a full list better than asking people in the street?","choices":["Everyone on the list has the same chance of being picked","It is quicker","It always gives the exact true value"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who walks down that street at that time.","explain":"Random selection from a full list gives every person the same chance, so no group is left out by the method. It still has random error."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Out of 50 forms sent, {k} came back. What percentage replied?","answer":"2*k","hint":"Each form is 2% of 50, so double the number that came back.","explain":"{k} out of 50 is {=2*k}%. A low reply rate is a warning sign of possible non-response bias.","params":{"k":[5,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"The 1936 Literary Digest poll had about 2.4 million replies but picked the wrong election winner. What does this show?","choices":["A huge sample can still be biased","Big samples are always wrong","The question must have been leading"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the size of the poll protect it?","explain":"Its list and its replies over-represented richer voters. Size cuts random error but cannot fix bias in who is asked or who replies."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A survey about school dinners asks only students in the lunch queue. What kind of bias is this?","choices":["Sampling bias: who is asked","A leading question","Non-response bias"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is missing from the lunch queue?","explain":"Students who bring packed lunches are never asked, so the sample over-represents people who already buy school dinners."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A council posts {=100*n} survey forms and {=10*k}% of them come back. How many forms come back?","answer":"10*n*k","hint":"Find that percentage of the number of forms posted.","explain":"{=10*k}% of {=100*n} is {=10*n*k} forms. If the people who did not reply differ from those who did, the result has non-response bias.","params":{"n":[2,9],"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which question is neutral?","choices":["How would you rate the school canteen?","Don't you think the canteen is poor?","How bad is the canteen food?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A neutral question does not hint at the answer it wants.","explain":"'How would you rate' lets people give any view. The other two assume the canteen is poor, which leads the answer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sample of 20 people includes {t} teenagers. What percentage of the sample are teenagers?","answer":"5*t","hint":"Each person is 5% of a sample of 20.","explain":"{t} out of 20 is {=5*t}%. Compare it with the share of teenagers in the whole town to see whether they are over- or under-represented.","params":{"t":[2,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A biased survey is repeated with 10 times as many people, using the same method. What happens to the bias?","choices":["It stays: a bigger sample cuts random error, not bias","It disappears","It gets ten times smaller"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is bias caused by bad luck or by the method?","explain":"Bias comes from the method, so asking more people the same way gives the same lean. Only random error shrinks."}],"explain":{"kid":["A survey is only as good as its method. Bias is a fault in the method that pushes the answer the same way every time. It is not bad luck, and a bigger sample does not cure it.","Sampling bias comes from who you ask. Ask 200 people at a skate shop about a new skate park and most are teens who skate. The result comes out too high. A random sample from a full list of the town gives everyone the same chance.","A leading question pushes people to one answer. Compare 'Don't you agree the town badly needs a fun, safe skate park?' with 'Should the town build a skate park?' The people are the same. Only the words changed, and so did the result.","Non-response bias comes from who answers. If teens reply less often than adults, the replies hold too many adult views. Sending more forms does not help, because the same people still reply. Reminders that get replies from every group do help."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. GCSE Mathematics in England asks students to infer properties of populations from a sample and to know the limitations of sampling, and KS3 statistics includes designing surveys. In the US, Common Core 7.SP.A.1 states that generalisations from a sample are valid only if the sample is representative, and HSS-IC.B.3 asks students to explain how randomisation relates to sample surveys.","Three sources of bias are worth naming. Sampling or selection bias: the method or list leaves groups out or over-represents them, as in convenience samples and self-selected, voluntary-response polls. Response bias from wording: leading or loaded questions, which survey researchers such as Pew Research Center have shown can shift results by many percentage points. Non-response bias: the people who do not reply differ from those who do.","The key idea is that bias is systematic error, while sampling variability is random error. A larger sample shrinks random error but leaves bias untouched, so a huge biased survey can be confidently wrong: the 1936 Literary Digest poll had about 2.4 million replies and still picked the wrong winner of the US election. Common mistakes: thinking a big sample cannot be biased, calling any surprising result biased, and fixing the sample while keeping a leading question.","In the lab students move a survey stand between a skate shop, a library and a random draw from the town list, reword a leading question one phrase at a time, drag reply rates for teens and adults (and see that ten times as many forms changes nothing), sort six faulty surveys by kind of bias, and fix a plan until its estimate matches the census value of 52%. The lab's town and its figures are a model, not real survey data."]},"familyTip":"Next time a headline says '9 out of 10 people agree', ask your teenager three questions together: Who was asked? What exactly was the question? How many people did not reply? Try rewriting a slanted question from an advert or a school survey so it no longer hints at the answer.","keyIdea":"Bias is a fault in how a survey is done that pushes the result the same way every time. It has three common sources: who is asked (sampling bias, such as a convenience sample or a self-selected online poll), how they are asked (a leading or loaded question), and who replies (non-response bias, when the people who answer differ from those who do not). A bigger sample cuts random error but does not fix bias; each source needs its own fix: a random sample from a full list, neutral wording, and follow-ups until every group replies.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each part of the survey fair, or a source of bias? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair method","Source of bias"],"items":[["Names drawn at random from the full town list",0],["Asking people outside the skate shop",1],["\"Don't you agree the town badly needs…?\"",1],["\"Should the town build a skate park?\"",0],["Counting only the 15% who replied, mostly retired people",1],["Reminders until every group replies at a similar rate",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsurvey","caption":"Flagship lab: move a survey stand, reword a leading question, drag reply rates, sort faulty surveys and fix a plan.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_1MoKvPonsZ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Values 5, 6, 7, 8, 9. The 9 changes to 90. What happens to the median?","choices":["It stays 7","It goes up a lot","It becomes 90"],"answer":0,"hint":"The median is the middle value. Does the middle change?","explain":"The middle value is still 7. The mean rises from 7 to 23.2, but the median stays 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The mean of 4 numbers is {m}. A fifth number, {x}, is added. What is the new mean?","answer":"(4*m+x)/5","hint":"Work out the old total first: mean × 4. Then add {x} and divide by 5.","explain":"Old total = 4 × {m} = {=4*m}. New total = {=4*m+x}. New mean = {=4*m+x} ÷ 5 = {=(4*m+x)/5}.","params":{"m":[10,20],"x":[50,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Heights: 150, 152, 153, 155 and 1540 cm. What should you do with 1540?","choices":["Check it: it is probably a typing error for 154","Keep it and use the mean","Always delete the largest value"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could a person really be 1540 cm tall?","explain":"1540 cm is over 15 m, so it is almost certainly an error. Check it; remove or correct it only for a reason like this."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Lap times 14, 15, 15, 16, 17 seconds. Is 17 an outlier?","choices":["No, it is close to the rest","Yes, it is the largest value","Yes, because it appears once"],"answer":0,"hint":"An outlier is far from the rest, not just the biggest.","explain":"17 is only 1 second above 16. Every set has a largest value; it is an outlier only if it sits far away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Data: 1, 3, 3, 5 and {x}, where {x} is bigger than 5. What is the median?","answer":"3","hint":"Order the five values and pick the third one.","explain":"In order: 1, 3, 3, 5, {x}. The third value is 3, so the median is 3 for any {x} above 5.","params":{"x":[6,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A shop wants to know which shoe size to order most of. Which average?","choices":["the mode","the mean","the median"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which average tells you the size that sells most often?","explain":"The mode is the most common size. A mean such as 5.7 is not a size the shop can order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Staff pay (£ thousand): 20, 22, 22, 25, 26, 300. An advert says 'average pay £69.2k'. Which average did it use?","choices":["the mean","the median","the mode"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add up all six values and divide by 6.","explain":"415 ÷ 6 ≈ 69.2, so it is the mean, pulled up by the 300. The median is 23.5 and the mode is 22."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Marks 61, 64, 66, 67, 70, all close together. Which average uses every mark?","choices":["the mean","the median","the mode"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which average adds up all the values?","explain":"The mean uses every mark, and with no outliers it is a fair typical value: 328 ÷ 5 = 65.6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Quiz marks 4, 15, 16, 17, 17, 18, 19, 19. The tail is on the low side. How do the averages compare?","choices":["The mean is lower than the median","The mean is higher than the median","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 4 pulls one average down. Which one uses every value?","explain":"The mean is 125 ÷ 8 ≈ 15.6 and the median is 17. The low 4 pulls the mean below the median."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which of these is NOT an average?","choices":["the range","the median","the mode"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these measures spread, not a typical value.","explain":"The range is largest minus smallest, a measure of spread. The mean, median and mode are averages."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Data: 3, 4, 4, 5, 60. Which average best shows a typical value?","choices":["the median","the mean","the largest value"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a value far from the rest? Which average does that value pull?","explain":"60 is an outlier. The mean is 76 ÷ 5 = 15.2, bigger than four of the five values. The median, 4, is typical."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The mean of 5 values is {m}. One value goes up by {d} and the rest stay the same. What is the new mean?","answer":"m+d/5","hint":"The total goes up by {d}. Share that extra out between the 5 values.","explain":"The total rises by {d}, so the mean rises by {d} ÷ 5. New mean = {m} + {=d/5} = {=m+d/5}.","params":{"m":[10,20],"d":[5,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which average can you find for the favourite colours of a class?","choices":["the mode","the mean","the median"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you add colours or put them in order?","explain":"Colours are categories, not numbers, so there is no mean or median. The mode is the most common colour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The data is 2, 4, 6, 8 and {x}. Here {x} is the largest value. What is the median?","answer":"6","hint":"Put the five values in order. Which one is in the middle?","explain":"In order: 2, 4, 6, 8, {x}. The middle value is 6, whatever the size of {x}, so the median is 6.","params":{"x":[9,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Pay in a firm: 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, 90 (£ thousand). Which is true?","choices":["The mean is higher than the median","The mean equals the median","The median is higher than the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"The data has a long tail of high values. Which average does the tail pull?","explain":"The mean is 239 ÷ 8 ≈ 29.9 and the median is 21.5. The 90 pulls the mean up."}],"explain":{"kid":["An average is one value that sums up a set of data. There are three. The mean is the total shared out equally. The median is the middle value when the data is in order. The mode is the value that comes up most often.","An outlier is a value far away from the rest. Take the pocket money 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7, 7, 8 and 9. The mean is about 6.4 and the median is 7. Change the 9 to 40. The mean jumps to about 9.9, more than all but one of the values. The median is still 7.","The mean uses every value, so one huge value drags it. The median only cares about the middle, so it hardly moves. When data has a long tail on one side, it is skewed, and the mean is pulled towards the tail.","So pick with care. Values close together with no outliers: use the mean, because it uses every value. Outliers or skew, like house prices or pay: use the median. Most common value, or categories like favourite fruit: use the mode. Check an outlier before you remove it. Only take it out if it is a mistake."],"adult":["Ages 12 to 14. The KS3 programme of study in England asks pupils to describe, interpret and compare distributions using appropriate measures of central tendency (mean, mode, median) and spread, including outliers. Common Core 6.SP.5c and 6.SP.5d ask students to relate the choice of measure of centre to the shape of the distribution and the context.","The mean is not resistant: changing one value by d changes the mean of n values by d/n, however extreme the value. The median is resistant: changing the largest value never moves it. In a right-skewed distribution such as income the mean usually lies above the median, and in a left-skewed one below it. The mode is the only average for categorical data and may not exist or may not be unique.","Common mistakes: always choosing the mean; thinking the largest or smallest value is automatically an outlier; deleting outliers without a reason (an outlier should be checked, and removed only if it is an error); thinking the median moves when an extreme value grows; expecting the mean and median to be equal in skewed data; and calling the range an average.","In the lab the learner drags the top value of a pocket money set from 9 to 30 or more and watches the mean move while the median stays at 7, decides whether each of three dot plots has an outlier, places the mean of a right-skewed and a left-skewed set by eye and checks it, matches six contexts to the best average, and decodes which average each of three true claims about one firm's pay used."]},"keyIdea":"The mean, the median and the mode each answer the question 'what is a typical value?' in a different way, so choose the one that suits the data. The mean uses every value, so a single outlier, a value far from the rest, can pull it a long way. The median is the middle value when the data is in order, so it barely moves when an extreme value changes. In skewed data the mean is pulled towards the long tail. Use the mean when values are close together with no outliers, the median when there are outliers or skew (house prices, pay), and the mode for the most common value or for categories such as favourite fruit.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which average gives the most useful typical value? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["mean","median","mode"],"items":[["Pay in a firm with a very highly paid boss",1],["Most popular shoe size to stock",2],["Five similar lap times",0],["House prices in a street with one mansion",1],["Favourite sport of a class",2],["Heights of pupils, no outliers",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagoutlr","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch one value and watch the mean and median, hunt for outliers, place the mean of skewed data, and decode claims.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_7x4MgNuBd8":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In a survey of 80, 18 are Year 7s who prefer sport. What is the joint relative frequency of Year 7 and sport?","choices":["18/80","18/30","18/38"],"answer":0,"hint":"Joint means out of everyone.","explain":"Joint relative frequency divides by the grand total: 18/80 = 0.225. 18/30 is conditional on Year 7, and 18/38 is conditional on sport."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The two column totals of a two-way table are {a} and {b}. What is the grand total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Every person is in exactly one column.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The row totals must add to the same number.","params":{"a":[20,60],"b":[20,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"42 students prefer music; 30 of them are in Year 9. What fraction of music fans are in Year 9?","choices":["30/42","30/50","30/80"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Of music fans' tells you to divide by the music column total.","explain":"Out of the 42 music fans, 30 are Year 9: 30/42 ≈ 0.71. 30/50 answers a different question: the share of Year 9s who like music."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Of 25 Year 10s asked, {a} have a part-time job. What percentage of these Year 10s have a job?","answer":"a*4","hint":"Divide by 25, then multiply by 100.","explain":"{a} ÷ 25 × 100 = {=a*4}%. The bottom of the fraction is the Year 10 total, because the question is about Year 10s.","params":{"a":[1,24]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"70% of dog owners walk daily but only 20% of non-owners do. What can you conclude?","choices":["Dog ownership and walking daily are associated","Owning a dog makes people walk","Nothing, the groups are different"],"answer":0,"hint":"A table shows whether percentages differ. Does it show why?","explain":"70% and 20% differ, so the two are associated. The table cannot show cause: people who already walk may choose to get dogs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"40% of the 30 Year 7s and 40% of the 50 Year 9s prefer music. Is year group associated with preferring music?","choices":["No","Yes, more Year 9s prefer music","Yes, the groups are different sizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the percentages, not the counts.","explain":"Both rows are 40%, so there is no association. 20 Year 9s is more than 12 Year 7s only because more Year 9s were asked."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Year 9 row: sport ?, music 30, total 50. Sport column total is 38. Which is the missing cell?","choices":["20","8","68"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the row: the two Year 9 cells add to 50.","explain":"50 − 30 = 20. Check with the column: Year 7 sport is 38 − 20 = 18. 68 adds instead of subtracting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why do row percentages and column percentages give different answers?","choices":["They divide by different totals","One of them is always wrong","They count different people"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what goes on the bottom of each fraction.","explain":"Row percentages divide by a row total and column percentages by a column total, so they answer different questions about the same cell."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Year 7 row: sport 18, music ?, total 30. What is the missing cell?","choices":["12","48","18"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two cells in a row add up to the row total.","explain":"18 + ? = 30, so ? = 30 − 18 = 12. Adding 18 to 30 counts the sport fans twice."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A row total is {t}. One cell in that row is {a}. What is the other cell?","answer":"t-a","hint":"The two cells add to the row total, so subtract the cell you know.","explain":"{t} − {a} = {=t-a}. The row total already includes {a}.","params":{"t":[30,60],"a":[5,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"80 students: 30 are Year 9s who like music, and there are 50 Year 9s. What fraction of Year 9s like music?","choices":["30/50","30/80","50/80"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Of Year 9s' tells you which total goes on the bottom.","explain":"Out of the 50 Year 9s, 30 like music: 30/50 = 0.6. 30/80 is the joint relative frequency, and 50/80 is the share who are in Year 9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Of 50 Year 8s, {a} cycle to school. What percentage of the Year 8s cycle?","answer":"a*2","hint":"Divide by the Year 8 total, then multiply by 100.","explain":"{a} ÷ 50 × 100 = {=a*2}%. This is a conditional relative frequency: out of the Year 8s only.","params":{"a":[5,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Of 20 left-handers, 6 wear glasses. Of 80 right-handers, 24 wear glasses. Is wearing glasses associated with handedness here?","choices":["No, both are 30%","Yes, 24 is more than 6","Yes, there are more right-handers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn each row into a percentage before you compare.","explain":"6/20 = 30% and 24/80 = 30%. The counts differ only because the groups differ in size, so there is no association."}],"explain":{"kid":["A two-way table sorts people by two things at once. The rows might be year groups and the columns what they chose. Every person goes in exactly one cell.","Row totals add across. Column totals add down. Both lead to the same grand total, so you can use totals to find a missing cell by subtracting.","A relative frequency is a count divided by a total. Out of everyone, divide by the grand total. Out of one group, such as the Year 9s, divide by that group's total.","To spot a link, compare percentages in each row. If 40% of Year 7s and 40% of Year 9s choose music, there is no link, even if more Year 9s were asked."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. Common Core 8.SP.4 asks students to build and read two-way tables of two categorical variables and to use row or column relative frequencies to describe a possible association. S-ID.5 names joint, marginal and conditional relative frequencies. GCSE Mathematics (AQA 8300, P and S strands) uses two-way tables for frequencies and probabilities.","A joint relative frequency divides a cell by the grand total. A marginal one divides a row or column total by the grand total. A conditional one divides a cell by its row or column total, and the wording tells you which: 'of the Year 9s' means the Year 9 row. Row and column percentages answer different questions, so 30/50 (Year 9s who like music) is not the same as 30/42 (music fans who are Year 9).","Common mistakes: dividing by the grand total when the question names a group, using the row total when the column is meant, adding a cell to a total instead of subtracting, judging association from raw counts when group sizes differ, and reading an association as a cause. Equal conditional relative frequencies in every row mean no association in that sample.","In the lab students sort eight survey cards into a 2 by 2 table, find four missing values from totals in an 80-student table, tap the total to divide by for four questions, drag the Year 9 music count until both rows show 40% (20 of 50), and judge three tables by row percentages."]},"familyTip":"Ask everyone at a family gathering two questions, like adult or child and tea or juice, and build the table together on paper. Then ask: what fraction of the children chose juice? And what fraction of everyone are children who chose juice? Talk about why the answers differ.","keyIdea":"A two-way table counts people by two categories at once, such as year group and favourite activity. Each person sits in exactly one cell; row totals add across, column totals add down, and both sets add to the grand total. A relative frequency is a count divided by a total. Divide by the grand total for a joint relative frequency (Year 9 and music, out of everyone). Divide by a row or column total for a conditional one (music, out of the Year 9s). Two variables are associated when the conditional relative frequencies differ between rows; compare percentages, not counts, because groups differ in size.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What do you divide by? Drag each question, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["grand total","a row total","a column total"],"items":[["Fraction of all students who are Year 9 and like music",0],["Fraction of Year 9s who like music",1],["Fraction of music fans who are Year 9",2],["Fraction of everyone who is Year 7 and likes sport",0],["Fraction of Year 7s who like sport",1],["Fraction of sport fans who are Year 7",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtwoway","caption":"Flagship lab: sort survey cards into a two-way table, fill gaps from totals, tap the right denominator, drag rows to remove an association, judge links.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Roy8xE8n4T":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Bags of flour should hold 500 g on average. A buyer thinks they hold less. Which hypotheses?","choices":["H₀: μ = 500, H₁: μ < 500","H₀: x̄ = 500, H₁: x̄ < 500","H₀: μ = 500, H₁: μ ≤ 500"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the population mean, and keep the H₀ value out of H₁.","explain":"μ is the population mean, so H₀: μ = 500 and H₁: μ < 500. x̄ is the sample mean, and H₁ cannot include 500 itself."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Is there any correlation between hours of sleep and test score? Which hypotheses?","choices":["H₀: ρ = 0, H₁: ρ ≠ 0","H₀: r = 0, H₁: r ≠ 0","H₀: ρ = 0, H₁: ρ > 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Any correlation has no direction. Use the population symbol.","explain":"ρ is the population correlation; r comes from the sample. 'Any correlation' gives a two-tailed H₁: ρ ≠ 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A dice gives 28 sixes in 120 rolls. Someone thinks it shows sixes less often than a fair dice. Which H₀?","choices":["p = 1/6","p = 28/120","p < 1/6"],"answer":0,"hint":"H₀ is an equality at the fair value.","explain":"A fair dice has p = 1/6, so H₀: p = 1/6. 28/120 is the sample result, and p < 1/6 is H₁."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these is a valid alternative hypothesis when H₀: p = 0.3?","choices":["p > 0.3","p ≥ 0.3","p̂ > 0.3"],"answer":0,"hint":"H₁ must be about the parameter and must not include 0.3.","explain":"p > 0.3 is valid. p ≥ 0.3 includes the H₀ value, and p̂ is a sample statistic."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A sample gives p̂ = 0.18 when H₀ is p = 0.25. The question asked whether p has changed. Which H₁?","choices":["p ≠ 0.25","p < 0.25","p > 0.25"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question, not the sample, decides the direction.","explain":"'Has changed' has no direction, so H₁: p ≠ 0.25. The sample being below 0.25 does not make the test one-tailed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is a parameter?","choices":["μ, the mean height of all students in a school","x̄ = 162 cm for 25 students","r = 0.6 for 15 pairs"],"answer":0,"hint":"A parameter describes the whole population.","explain":"μ describes the whole population. x̄ and r are calculated from samples, so they are statistics."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"H₀: p = 0.5 for a coin. It is tossed {=2*m} times. How many heads does H₀ lead you to expect?","answer":"m","hint":"Expected number = n × p under H₀.","explain":"{=2*m} × 0.5 = {m} heads are expected if H₀ is true.","params":{"m":[10,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A claim says 1 in {k} special dice rolls shows a star, so H₀: p = 1/{k}. In {=k*m} rolls, how many stars does H₀ lead you to expect?","answer":"m","hint":"Multiply the number of rolls by the H₀ probability.","explain":"{=k*m} × 1/{k} = {m} stars expected under H₀.","params":{"k":[4,10],"m":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A rival suspects that more than 10% of a firm's parts are faulty. The firm claims 10%. Which hypotheses?","choices":["H₀: p = 0.1, H₁: p > 0.1","H₀: p = 0.1, H₁: p < 0.1","H₀: p > 0.1, H₁: p = 0.1"],"answer":0,"hint":"The firm's figure goes in H₀; the rival's suspicion sets H₁.","explain":"H₀: p = 0.1 is the firm's claim and H₁: p > 0.1 is the rival's suspicion of more faults. The equality never goes in H₁."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A drug company claims its drug helps more than 40% of patients. In a trial, 52 of 100 are helped. Which hypotheses test the claim?","choices":["H₀: p = 0.4, H₁: p > 0.4","H₀: p = 0.52, H₁: p > 0.52","H₀: p > 0.4, H₁: p = 0.4"],"answer":0,"hint":"H₀ uses the claimed value as an equality. The claim's direction goes in H₁.","explain":"H₀: p = 0.4 is the value being tested and H₁: p > 0.4 is the claim of more than 40%. 0.52 is the sample proportion, and the equality always goes in H₀."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"H₀: p = 0.25 for a spinner landing on red. It is spun {=4*m} times. How many reds does H₀ lead you to expect?","answer":"m","hint":"Under H₀ the expected number is n × p.","explain":"{=4*m} × 0.25 = {m} reds expected if H₀ is true. A result far from {m} is evidence against H₀.","params":{"m":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ali thinks a coin is biased but does not say which way. Which H₁?","choices":["p ≠ 0.5","p > 0.5","p < 0.5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the claim give a direction?","explain":"Biased with no direction means p could be above or below 0.5, so H₁: p ≠ 0.5, a two-tailed test."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A firm claims {a}% of customers reuse its bags. You test the claim. Under H₀, how many out of 100 customers do you expect to reuse bags?","answer":"a","hint":"H₀ takes the firm's claimed proportion as true.","explain":"H₀: p = {a}/100, so 100 × {a}/100 = {a} customers are expected to reuse bags.","params":{"a":[20,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do hypotheses use p and not the sample proportion p̂?","choices":["The test is about the unknown population value; p̂ is the evidence","p̂ is too hard to calculate","p̂ is only used in two-tailed tests"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one do you already know, and which one is the question about?","explain":"You already know p̂ from the sample. The question is about p, the population proportion, which you cannot see directly; p̂ is the evidence used to judge it."}],"explain":{"kid":["A hypothesis test asks whether a sample is strong enough evidence against a claim about a whole population. The claim is about a parameter: p for a proportion, μ for a mean, ρ for a correlation. You never know it exactly. What you know is a statistic from your sample, like p̂ = 21/60 = 0.35. The statistic is your evidence, so it never goes in a hypothesis.","The null hypothesis H₀ says nothing unusual is going on, so the parameter equals the claimed value. A spinner that should land on red 25% of the time gives H₀: p = 0.25. H₀ is always an equality, because the test works out probabilities assuming exactly that value.","The alternative H₁ says how you think the parameter differs. Read the claim. If someone suspects red comes up more often, H₁: p > 0.25. If they suspect less often, H₁: p < 0.25. Both are one-tailed. If they only think the spinner is biased, with no direction, H₁: p ≠ 0.25, which is two-tailed.","Choose H₁ before you look at the data. Getting 0.35 in your sample does not mean H₁ should be p > 0.25; the question you were asked decides that. And H₁ never includes the H₀ value, so write p > 0.25, not p ≥ 0.25."],"adult":["Ages 16 to 18. This is A level Statistics. At AS, students test a binomial proportion. At A level, they also test the mean of a normal model and a correlation. IB and AP Statistics cover the same skill. Students must state hypotheses about the population for a given context. They must also pick a one-tailed or two-tailed test from the wording.","The terms: a parameter (p, μ, ρ) is a fixed number for the whole population, and we do not know it. A statistic (p̂, x̄, r) is worked out from a sample, and it changes from sample to sample. H₀ is an equality, because the test needs one exact value to find probabilities, such as X ~ B(n, 0.25). H₁ is one-tailed (< or >) or two-tailed (≠). It is fixed before the data come in.","Common mistakes: using the sample value (H₀: p = 0.35), using a sample symbol (H₁: x̄ < 500), putting an inequality in H₀, swapping H₀ and H₁, and using ≥ or ≤ in H₁. Others pick the tail after seeing which way the data went, or write a count for p (H₀: p = 21). Mark schemes also want p defined in context, such as the chance the spinner lands on red.","In the lab students sort eight cards into parameters and statistics. They drag the claimed value into H₀ for three contexts, and drag <, > or ≠ into H₁ for four claims. They shade the values of p that H₁ allows on a number line; tapping the H₀ point gets feedback. Last, they pick the one correct pair of hypotheses for a proportion, a mean and a correlation."]},"familyTip":"Make up a claim together, such as 'this cereal box toy is in 1 in 4 boxes'. Ask your teenager to write H₀ and H₁ if they suspect the toy is rarer than that, then if they just think the claim is wrong. Then ask why the hypotheses do not use the number they found in their own boxes.","keyIdea":"A hypothesis test starts with two statements about a population parameter, such as the proportion p, the mean μ or the correlation ρ, never about a sample statistic such as p̂, x̄ or r. The null hypothesis H₀ is an equality at the claimed or 'nothing unusual' value (H₀: p = 0.25). The alternative hypothesis H₁ says how the parameter differs, and its direction comes from the claim, decided before the data are seen: 'more' gives H₁: p > 0.25, 'less' gives p < 0.25 (both one-tailed), and 'different' with no direction gives p ≠ 0.25 (two-tailed). H₁ never includes the H₀ value, so ≥ and ≤ are not used.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which quantities can appear in a hypothesis? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["parameter: can go in H₀ or H₁","statistic: never in H₀ or H₁"],"items":[["p, the proportion of all voters",0],["p̂ = 0.35 from 60 spins",1],["μ, the mean of all bags",0],["x̄ = 498 g from 30 bags",1],["ρ, the population correlation",0],["r = 0.42 from 20 pairs",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnullh","caption":"Flagship lab: sort parameters from statistics, drag the value into H₀, drag the sign into H₁, shade H₁ on a line, pick the correct pair.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_y6FdfXzHiq":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Red comes up 9 times in 40 spins. H₀: p = 0.3. What is the null distribution?","choices":["X ~ B(40, 0.3)","X ~ B(40, 0.225)","X ~ B(9, 0.3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the number of spins and the p in H₀.","explain":"n = 40 spins and p₀ = 0.3 from H₀. 0.225 = 9/40 is the sample proportion, which is the evidence, not the model."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is the null distribution worked out assuming H₀ is true?","choices":["To see how likely the observed result would be if H₀ held","Because H₀ has been proved true","Because the data always agree with H₀"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the test need a fixed model for?","explain":"H₀ is assumed, not known. The model shows which results would be ordinary or rare if H₀ held, so the data can be judged against it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In a test of whether over 2% of bolts are faulty, 50 bolts are checked. What is the test statistic?","choices":["X, the number of faulty bolts in the 50","p = 0.02","The time until the first faulty bolt"],"answer":0,"hint":"A binomial test counts successes in a fixed number of trials.","explain":"X counts faulty bolts in the sample of 50. 0.02 is the H₀ value of the parameter, and a waiting time is not a binomial count."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The same data are tested against H₀: p = 0.3 instead of H₀: p = 0.5. What changes?","choices":["The null distribution","The observed value of X","The number of trials"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which of these depends on H₀?","explain":"The null distribution is B(n, p₀), so a new p₀ moves it. The data and n were fixed by the experiment."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Under H₀, X ~ B({=10*m}, 0.1). What is the expected value of X?","answer":"m","hint":"For X ~ B(n, p), the expected value is E(X) = np.","explain":"np = {=10*m} × 0.1 = {m}.","params":{"m":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A spinner should land on blue with probability 0.25. It is spun {=4*m} times to test this. What is the mean of X under H₀?","answer":"m","hint":"Write X ~ B(n, 0.25), then use np.","explain":"X ~ B({=4*m}, 0.25), so the mean is {=4*m} × 0.25 = {m}.","params":{"m":[5,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Under H₀: p = 0.5, X ~ B(20, 0.5) and P(X ≥ 15) ≈ 0.021. What does this mean?","choices":["15 or more heads would be rare if the coin were fair","The coin has a 2.1% chance of being fair","H₀ is true 97.9% of the time"],"answer":0,"hint":"The probability is worked out assuming H₀.","explain":"It is the chance of 15 or more heads if H₀ is true. It is not the probability that H₀ is true."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"5 cards are dealt from one pack without replacement and the hearts counted. Why is B(5, 1/4) not exact?","choices":["The chance of a heart changes after each card, so trials are not independent","There are more than two suits","5 is too few trials for any binomial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does each card have the same chance of being a heart?","explain":"Without replacement the chance of a heart changes as cards are dealt, so the trials are not independent with constant p."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is the best definition of the null distribution?","choices":["The distribution of the test statistic if H₀ is true","The distribution of the sample data you collected","The distribution of p after the test"],"answer":0,"hint":"Null refers to H₀. Which option describes the statistic under H₀?","explain":"It is how the test statistic would vary from sample to sample if H₀ were true, such as X ~ B(20, 0.5)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A dice is rolled 60 times to test whether it shows six too often. What is the test statistic?","choices":["X, the number of sixes in the 60 rolls","p, the probability of a six","60, the number of rolls"],"answer":0,"hint":"The statistic is calculated from the sample. Which of these is counted?","explain":"X counts sixes in the sample. p is the parameter H₀ makes a claim about, and 60 is the fixed number of trials."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Under H₀, X ~ B({=2*m}, 0.5). What is the mean of the null distribution?","answer":"m","hint":"The mean of B(n, p) is np.","explain":"np = {=2*m} × 0.5 = {m}. If H₀ is true, counts near {m} are ordinary.","params":{"m":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"60 rolls give 15 sixes. H₀: p = 1/6. What is the distribution of X under H₀?","choices":["X ~ B(60, 1/6)","X ~ B(60, 1/4)","X ~ B(15, 1/6)"],"answer":0,"hint":"n is the number of trials; p comes from H₀.","explain":"There are 60 trials and H₀ says p = 1/6, so X ~ B(60, 1/6). 1/4 is the sample proportion 15/60, and 15 is the observed count."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Under H₀, X ~ B({=4*m}, 0.5). What is the variance of X?","answer":"m","hint":"The variance of B(n, p) is np(1 − p).","explain":"np(1 − p) = {=4*m} × 0.5 × 0.5 = {m}.","params":{"m":[3,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which count cannot be modelled by a binomial distribution?","choices":["The number of rolls needed to get the first six","The number of heads in 50 coin tosses","The number of 40 patients who recover, each independently with chance 0.7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check each condition: fixed n, two outcomes, independence, constant p.","explain":"Rolling until the first six has no fixed number of trials, so it is not binomial. The other two meet every condition."}],"explain":{"kid":["A hypothesis test needs one number from the sample to judge the claim. That number is the test statistic. In a binomial test it is X, the count of successes: the sixes in 60 rolls, or the faulty bolts in 50. The parameter p is not the statistic. It belongs to the whole population, and H₀ makes a claim about it.","Next, write down how X would behave if H₀ were true. This is the null distribution. For 60 rolls of a dice with H₀: p = 1/6, it is X ~ B(60, 1/6). The n is the number of trials. The p is the value in H₀, not the share you saw. If you rolled 15 sixes, 15/60 is your evidence, so it cannot also be the model you test it against.","Why assume H₀? Because we need a fixed model to measure surprise. If you simulate a fair coin tossed 20 times, again and again, most head counts land near np = 10. A count of 17 hardly ever happens, so seeing 17 would be strong evidence against a fair coin.","Check that a binomial model fits: a fixed number of trials, two outcomes, independent trials and the same p every time. Counting rolls until the first six fails, because n is not fixed. Changing H₀ moves the null distribution, but your data stay exactly where they were."],"adult":["Ages 16 to 18. This is A level Mathematics statistics: students state the test statistic and its distribution under H₀ for a binomial test, for example X ~ B(20, 0.5). It follows from writing H₀ and H₁, and leads on to p-values and critical regions, which are both tail probabilities of this distribution.","The terms. A test statistic is calculated from sample data. The null distribution is its sampling distribution when H₀ is true. For a binomial test, X ~ B(n, p₀) with mean np₀ and variance np₀(1 − p₀). It needs fixed n, two outcomes, independence and constant p. Sampling without replacement from a small population breaks independence.","Common mistakes. Students give the parameter p, or the number of trials, as the test statistic. They put the sample proportion into B(n, p) instead of p₀, or swap the observed count and n (B(15, 1/6)). Some think H₀ is assumed because it is true. Others forget to define X in context, which mark schemes require.","In the lab students choose the test statistic for four contexts and build the null distribution for three by tapping n and p. They run 300 simulations of a fair coin and watch them match B(20, 0.5). They sort six situations by whether a binomial model fits. Then they drag H₀'s p while the observed 14 stays put, and read P(X ≥ x) for 13, 15 and 17 heads."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager how they would test whether a dice rolls too many sixes. What would they count? What distribution would that count have if the dice were fair? Then ask why the distribution uses 1/6 and not the proportion of sixes they actually rolled.","keyIdea":"A test statistic is a quantity calculated from the sample that is used to test H₀. In a binomial test it is X, the number of successes in n trials, not the parameter p. Its null distribution is the distribution X would have if H₀ were true: X ~ B(n, p₀), where n is the number of trials and p₀ is the value stated in H₀, never the sample proportion. The binomial model needs a fixed n, two outcomes, independent trials and a constant p. The null distribution depends only on H₀ and n, so it tells you which results are ordinary and which would be rare if H₀ were true.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which numbers go into the null distribution X ~ B(n, p₀)? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["goes into B(n, p₀)","does not"],"items":[["n = 60 rolls",0],["p₀ = 1/6 from H₀",0],["15 sixes observed",1],["sample proportion 15/60",1],["n = 20 tosses",0],["5% significance level",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtstat","caption":"Flagship lab: pick the test statistic, build X ~ B(n, p₀), simulate H₀, check binomial conditions, move H₀ under fixed data, and read tails.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uS7h8Xqs6F":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does the interquartile range measure?","choices":["the spread of the middle half of the data","the middle value","the difference between largest and smallest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which two values the IQR uses.","explain":"IQR = Q3 − Q1, so it measures how spread out the middle half is. The middle value is the median, and largest − smallest is the range."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Data: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 14, 16, 20. What is the upper quartile?","choices":["14","13","16"],"answer":0,"hint":"With 11 values, the upper half is the 5 values above the median.","explain":"The median is 9, the 6th value. The upper half is 11, 13, 14, 16, 20, and its middle is 14, so Q3 = 14."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Q1 is {a} and Q3 is {b}. What is the IQR?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract the lower quartile from the upper quartile.","explain":"IQR = {b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[10,40],"b":[50,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An ordered data set has {n} values. Q1 is which value number in the list?","answer":"(n+1)/4","hint":"Use (n + 1)/4 for the lower quartile.","explain":"({n} + 1) ÷ 4 = {=(n+1)/4}, so Q1 is value number {=(n+1)/4} in the ordered list.","params":{"n":[7,43]},"where":"n%4==3"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Data: 4, 8, 10, 12, 30. Why is the IQR better than the range for describing spread here?","choices":["30 is an outlier, and the IQR ignores the extreme ends","The IQR is always bigger","The range cannot be found"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which value looks far away from the rest?","explain":"30 is far from the others, so the range (26) is stretched. The IQR uses only the middle half, so it is not pulled by one extreme value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Team A: median 20, IQR 3. Team B: median 20, IQR 11. Which statement is right?","choices":["Team A’s scores are more consistent","Team B’s scores are more consistent","The teams are exactly alike"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same median, so compare the spread.","explain":"The medians match, so typical scores are the same. Team A’s smaller IQR means its middle scores are closer together: more consistent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Jo finds Q1 for 2, 5, 6, 9, 20, 22, 26 as 2 + (26 − 2) ÷ 4 = 8. What went wrong?","choices":["Q1 is a position in the ordered list, not a quarter of the range","She forgot to order the data","Nothing: Q1 is 8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quartiles count values, not distance along the number line.","explain":"Q1 is the middle of the lower half: 2, 5, 6, so Q1 = 5. Adding a quarter of the range finds a point on the number line, not a quartile."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Data: 7, 2, 9, 4, 11, 5, 8. What is the IQR?","choices":["5","9","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sort first, then find Q1 and Q3.","explain":"In order: 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11. Q1 = 4 and Q3 = 9, so IQR = 9 − 4 = 5. 9 is the range (11 − 2) and 3 is not a quartile gap."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Data in order: 2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 12. What is the lower quartile?","choices":["4","5","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Q1 is the middle of the lower half: the values below the median.","explain":"The median is 7, the 4th value. The lower half is 2, 4, 5, and its middle is 4, so Q1 = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The lower quartile of a data set is {a} and the upper quartile is {b}. What is the interquartile range?","answer":"b-a","hint":"IQR = upper quartile − lower quartile.","explain":"IQR = {b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[5,20],"b":[30,60]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A data set has {n} values in order. Which position is the median? (Give the value number.)","answer":"(n+1)/2","hint":"The median is the (n + 1)/2 th value.","explain":"({n} + 1) ÷ 2 = {=(n+1)/2}, so the median is value number {=(n+1)/2} in the ordered list.","params":{"n":[7,31]},"where":"n%2==1"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Data: 15, 3, 9, 12, 6, 18, 21. What is the median?","choices":["12","3","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put the values in order before you look for the middle.","explain":"In order: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21. The middle (4th) value is 12. The 4th value of the unsorted list is 12 by luck here, but always sort first."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A class’s quiz marks have an IQR of 6. One pupil scores 0. What happens?","choices":["The range grows, but the IQR barely changes","The IQR grows a lot","Both stay exactly the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The IQR only uses the middle half of the data.","explain":"A score of 0 stretches the range because it is a new smallest value. Q1 and Q3 move little or not at all, so the IQR barely changes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Quartiles split data into four equal parts. First, put the values in order. The middle value is the median. It splits the data into a lower half and an upper half.","The lower quartile, Q1, is the middle of the lower half. The upper quartile, Q3, is the middle of the upper half. If there is an odd number of values, leave the median out of both halves.","The interquartile range is Q3 minus Q1. It tells you how spread out the middle half of the data is. A small IQR means the values are close together, so the data are consistent.","The range is largest minus smallest, so one very big or very small value changes it a lot. The IQR ignores the ends, so it hardly changes. That makes the IQR a fairer measure of spread when there are outliers."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. In England this is the KS3 and GCSE statistics content: interpret, analyse and compare distributions through measures of central tendency (median) and spread (range, quartiles and interquartile range), leading to box plots and cumulative frequency. In the US it matches Common Core 6.SP.B.5c and 7.SP.B.3–4 (IQR as a measure of variability) and S-ID.A.2.","Method used here: order the n values; the median is the (n + 1)/2 th value; Q1 is the median of the values below the median and Q3 of those above it, which for small sets matches the GCSE (n + 1)/4 th and 3(n + 1)/4 th positions. The lab uses 7 and 11 values, where these methods agree exactly. Other methods (including the median in each half, or calculators’ interpolation) can give slightly different quartiles for other n, so check which method an exam board or calculator uses.","IQR = Q3 − Q1 measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data. Because it ignores the lowest and highest quarters, it is resistant to outliers, while the range is not. To compare distributions, quote a measure of average (median) and a measure of spread (IQR) in context: a smaller IQR means more consistent values.","Common mistakes: finding quartiles from unsorted data; taking Q1 as a quarter of the way along the range (smallest + range ÷ 4) instead of a quarter of the way through the ordered list; counting the median in a half and so landing one place off; giving the range when asked for the IQR; and saying a bigger IQR is better or more consistent."]},"familyTip":"Write down how many minutes each person at home spent on a phone or reading yesterday, or use a sports team’s scores. Put them in order together, find the median, then Q1 and Q3. Ask: who is typical, and how spread out is the middle half?","keyIdea":"Put the data in order first. The median cuts the ordered list in half. The lower quartile (Q1) is the median of the lower half and the upper quartile (Q3) is the median of the upper half; with an odd number of values, leave the median out of both halves. For n values, Q1 is the (n + 1)/4 th value and Q3 the 3(n + 1)/4 th. The interquartile range is IQR = Q3 − Q1: the spread of the middle half of the data. Unlike the range (largest − smallest), the IQR ignores the extreme ends, so one outlier barely changes it. Compare groups with the median for a typical value and the IQR for consistency.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Range or IQR: which one does each fact describe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["the range","the IQR"],"items":[["largest − smallest",0],["Q3 − Q1",1],["changes a lot with one outlier",0],["spread of the middle half",1],["uses only the two extreme values",0],["the width of the box in a box plot",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagiqr","caption":"Flagship lab: order the data, tap the median and quartiles, stretch a box from Q1 to Q3, drag an outlier, and compare two players.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bESTSBB0wK":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"On a temperature line graph, how do you find when it warmed up fastest?","choices":["Find the steepest part going up","Find the highest point","Find the longest part of the line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fast change means a big rise in a short time.","explain":"The steepest upward part shows the biggest rise in the shortest time. The highest point only shows when it was warmest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Each grid line is worth {s} °C. A point is 4 lines above 10 °C. What does it show? (Type the number.)","answer":"10+4*s","hint":"Start at 10 and count up 4 steps of {s}.","explain":"4 × {s} = {=4*s}, and 10 + {=4*s} = {=10+4*s} °C.","params":{"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"At noon it was {n} °C. At 6 am it was {m} °C. How much warmer was it at noon? (Type the number.)","answer":"n-m","hint":"How much warmer means the difference. Subtract.","explain":"{n} − {m} = {=n-m} °C warmer.","params":{"n":[18,24],"m":[4,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which data should go on a line graph?","choices":["A plant's height each week","Pets owned by each child","Favourite sports in a class"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one changes over time?","explain":"The plant grows between measurements, so its height suits a line graph. Pets and sports are groups, so they need a bar chart."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Between 2 pm and 4 pm the graph is flat at 22 °C. What was the temperature at 3 pm?","choices":["22 °C","44 °C","11 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flat means it stayed the same.","explain":"The line stays at 22 °C from 2 pm to 4 pm, so at 3 pm it was 22 °C. Do not add or halve the readings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Nobody read the thermometer at 1 pm. It was 20 °C at noon and 22 °C at 2 pm. What can you say about 1 pm?","choices":["Read the line: about 21 °C","Nothing: it was not measured","It must be 20 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"The line fills the gap between readings.","explain":"Halfway between noon and 2 pm the line is halfway between 20 and 22, so about 21 °C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"From 4 pm to 6 pm the line falls from 22 °C to 14 °C. What happened?","choices":["It cooled by 8 °C","It warmed by 8 °C","It cooled by 36 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Going down means falling. Find the difference.","explain":"The line goes down, so it cooled. 22 − 14 = 8 °C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is time put along the bottom of a temperature line graph?","choices":["Time goes on steadily and the temperature changes with it","Time is always the bigger number","It does not matter which way round"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one depends on the other?","explain":"Time moves on steadily, and the temperature changes as it does. Putting time along the bottom lets you read the change from left to right."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"When should the points of a graph be joined with a line?","choices":["When the data changes over time","Whenever there are points","When the bars are tall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there anything in between the readings?","explain":"Join points only when the data changes smoothly over time, like temperature. Separate groups get a bar chart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Each grid line on a line graph is worth {s} °C. A point is 3 lines above 0. What temperature does it show? (Type the number.)","answer":"3*s","hint":"Count up in steps of {s}, not 1.","explain":"3 lines × {s} °C = {=3*s} °C. Always check what one grid line is worth.","params":{"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"At 8 am it was {a} °C. At 10 am it was {=a+2*r} °C, and the line between is straight. What was the temperature at 9 am? (Type the number.)","answer":"a+r","hint":"9 am is halfway between 8 am and 10 am. Find the number halfway between the two readings.","explain":"The rise is {=2*r} °C, so halfway is {r} °C up: {a} + {r} = {=a+r} °C.","params":{"a":[4,12],"r":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Part of a temperature graph is flat and high up. What does it show?","choices":["The temperature stayed the same","It was 0 °C","It was getting hotter fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flat means the line does not go up or down.","explain":"A flat line means no change. It can be high up and still flat: the temperature stayed where it was."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A line graph rises to 18 °C at 11 am and falls back through 18 °C at 5 pm. When was it 18 °C?","choices":["At 11 am and at 5 pm","Only at 11 am","Only at 5 pm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw across from 18 °C. How many times does it meet the line?","explain":"The line crosses 18 °C twice: once warming up at 11 am and once cooling down at 5 pm."}],"explain":{"kid":["A line graph shows how something changes over time. Time goes along the bottom. The amount goes up the side. Each reading is a dot. Then you join the dots in time order.","We can join the dots because the temperature changes smoothly between readings. So the line lets you read a time that nobody measured. At 9 am, halfway between 10 °C and 16 °C, the line is at 13 °C.","The shape tells a story. A steep line means a fast change. A flat line means no change, even if it is high up. A line going down means the amount fell. One value can come up twice, once on the way up and once on the way down.","Check the scale before you read. Each grid line might be worth 2 or 5, not 1. And only join dots for data that changes over time. Separate groups, like favourite fruits, need a bar chart."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11. The National Curriculum in England asks Year 5 pupils to solve problems using information in tables and line graphs, and Year 6 pupils to interpret and construct line graphs and use them to solve problems. CCSS 5.G.A.2 and 6.SP.B.4 cover plotting points and displaying numerical data. The lab uses one day of temperatures, from 8 °C at 6 am up to 22 °C in the afternoon and down to 14 °C at 6 pm.","Key ideas: the horizontal axis carries time and the vertical axis the measure. Points are joined because the quantity is continuous, so values between readings can be estimated (interpolation). The gradient of each segment shows the rate of change: steep is fast, horizontal is no change, downward is a fall. Reading backwards from a value can give more than one time. Categorical data, such as favourite fruit, is shown with separate bars that are never joined.","Common mistakes: reading the vertical scale as one unit per grid line when each line is worth 2; thinking the highest part of the graph is where it rose fastest; reading a flat line as zero or as missing data; giving only the first time a value occurs; adding two readings instead of reading between them; and joining points for categories. Each one has its own feedback in the lab and the challenge."]},"familyTip":"Check the outdoor temperature on a phone or thermometer every two hours on a weekend day and plot it together on squared paper. Then ask: when did it warm up fastest, was it ever flat, and what was it at a time you did not check?","keyIdea":"A line graph shows how something changes over time. Time goes along the bottom and the amount goes up the side. Plot each reading as a point, then join the points in time order, because the amount changes smoothly in between. That lets you read times nobody measured. A steep line means a fast change, a flat line means no change, and a line going down means it fell. Read the scale carefully: each grid line may be worth 2, 5 or 10, not 1. Only use a line graph for data that changes over time; separate groups need a bar chart.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Line graph or bar chart? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["line graph","bar chart"],"items":[["A puppy's weight each month",0],["Favourite colours in Class 6",1],["Water in a bath as it fills",0],["Votes for each school trip",1],["Your height on each birthday",0],["Books read by each pupil",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglinegr","caption":"Flagship lab: plot a day of temperatures, read times nobody measured, find steep, flat and falling parts, find both 18 °C times and pick line or bar.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
